1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,075
Chapter 59: Hematological Disorders.

3
00:00:06,075 --> 00:00:28,825
Iron-Deficiency Anemia Megaloblastic Anemia Hemolytic Anemia Aplastic And Hypoplastic Anemia Polycythemias Hemoglobinopathies Thalassemia Syndromes Platelet Disorders Thrombotic Microangiopathies Inherited Coagulation Defects References Pregnant women are susceptible to several hematological abnormalities that may affect any woman of childbearing age.

4
00:00:28,825 --> 00:00:37,550
These include chronic disorders such as hereditary anemias, immunological thrombocytopenia, and hematological malignancies.

5
00:00:37,550 --> 00:00:41,512
Other disorders arise from pregnancy-induced demands.

6
00:00:41,512 --> 00:00:45,600
Two are iron deficiency and megaloblastic anemias.

7
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:50,475
Pregnancy may also unmask underlying hematological conditions.

8
00:00:50,475 --> 00:00:58,612
Importantly, pregnancy induces physiological changes that often confuse diagnosis and assessment of these disorders .

9
00:00:58,612 --> 00:01:00,387
Anemias.

10
00:01:00,387 --> 00:01:03,262
# Definition and Incidence.

11
00:01:03,262 --> 00:01:11,675
Normal values for concentrations of many cellular elements during pregnancy are listed in the Appendix (p. 1227).

12
00:01:11,675 --> 00:01:29,062
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (1998) defined anemia in iron-supplemented pregnant women using a cutoff of the 5th percentile, which is 11 g/dL in the first and third trimesters and 10.5 g/dL in the second trimester.

13
00:01:29,062 --> 00:01:32,987
Notably, these were not based on a U.S. population.

14
00:01:32,987 --> 00:01:41,387
Table 59-1 describes the distribution of hematocrit values of 480 iron-sufficient women at Parkland Hospital .

15
00:01:41,387 --> 00:01:47,125
Using these data, values below 30 percent seem reasonable to define anemia.

16
00:01:47,125 --> 00:01:49,712
Table 59-1.

17
00:01:49,712 --> 00:02:01,287
Hematocrit Values in Pregnancy The modest fall in hemoglobin and hematocrit values during pregnancy stems from a relatively greater expansion of plasma volume compared with red cell volume .

18
00:02:01,287 --> 00:02:09,662
The disproportion between the rates at which plasma and erythrocytes add to the maternal circulation is greatest during the second trimester.

19
00:02:09,662 --> 00:02:16,725
Late in pregnancy, plasma expansion essentially ceases, while hemoglobin mass continues to accrue.

20
00:02:16,725 --> 00:02:23,025
The causes of more common anemias encountered in pregnancy are listed in Table 59-2.

21
00:02:23,025 --> 00:02:35,012
Their frequency is dependent on multiple factors such as geography, ethnicity, socioeconomic level, nutrition, preexisting iron status, and prenatal iron supplementation .

22
00:02:35,012 --> 00:02:40,950
In the United States, the prevalence of anemia in pregnancy is 3 to 38 percent .

23
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:43,512
Table 59-2.

24
00:02:43,512 --> 00:03:05,037
Causes of Anemia During Pregnancy Initial evaluation of a pregnant woman with moderate anemia includes measurements of hemoglobin, hematocrit, red cell indices, and serum iron or ferritin levels; careful examination of a peripheral blood smear; and a sicklecell preparation if the woman has African lineage (Appendix p. 1227).

25
00:03:05,037 --> 00:03:08,337
# Effects on Pregnancy Outcomes.

26
00:03:08,337 --> 00:03:19,287
Anemia is associated with several adverse pregnancy outcomes (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021; Rahmati, 2020).

27
00:03:19,287 --> 00:03:25,587
Most anemia studies during pregnancy describe large populations and nutritional anemias.

28
00:03:25,587 --> 00:03:37,437
In a Canadian study, 12 percent of more than 500,000 women had mild anemia defined by a hemoglobin concentration of 9.0 to 10.9 g/dL .

29
00:03:37,437 --> 00:03:42,637
These women had a 2.5-fold increased risk for blood transfusions.

30
00:03:42,637 --> 00:03:51,275
With moderate anemia, incidences of fetal-growth restriction, low 5-minute Apgar score, and perinatal mortality were increased.

31
00:03:51,275 --> 00:03:55,912
Ray and coworkers (2020) reported similar results.

32
00:03:55,912 --> 00:04:01,662
Correction of iron-deficiency anemia results in a lower transfusion rate with delivery .

33
00:04:01,662 --> 00:04:13,700
A seemingly paradoxical finding is that healthy pregnant women with higher hemoglobin concentrations also are at greater risk for adverse perinatal outcomes (von Tempelhoff, 2008).

34
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:21,262
This may result from lower than average plasma volume expansion of pregnancy concurrent with normal red cell mass accrual.

35
00:04:21,262 --> 00:04:30,900
Scanlon and associates (2000) studied the relationship between maternal hemoglobin levels and rates of preterm or growthrestricted newborns.

36
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:43,000
Women whose hemoglobin concentration was three standard deviations above the mean at 12 or 18 weeks’ gestation had a 1.3to 1.8-fold greater incidence of fetal-growth restriction.

37
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,212
Placental weight correlates negatively with maternal hemoglobin concentration .

38
00:04:48,212 --> 00:04:53,675
These findings have led some to the illogical conclusion that withholding iron to cause

39
00:04:53,836 --> 00:04:58,073
iron-deficiency anemia will improve pregnancy outcomes .

40
00:04:58,023 --> 00:05:01,036
# Iron-deficiency Anemia.

41
00:05:01,036 --> 00:05:08,498
The two most common causes of anemia during pregnancy and the puerperium are iron deficiency and acute blood loss .

42
00:05:08,498 --> 00:05:15,398
In a typical singleton gestation, the maternal need for iron averages nearly 1000 mg .

43
00:05:15,398 --> 00:05:19,873
Multifetal gestational requirements are considerably higher .

44
00:05:19,873 --> 00:05:27,711
These amounts exceed the iron stores of most women and result in iron-deficiency anemia unless supplementation is provided.

45
00:05:27,711 --> 00:05:35,311
In the third trimester, additional iron is needed to augment maternal hemoglobin levels and for transport to the fetus.

46
00:05:35,311 --> 00:05:46,211
Because the amount of iron diverted to the fetus is similar in a normal and in an iron-deficient mother, the newborn of a severely anemic mother does not suffer from iron-deficiency anemia.

47
00:05:46,211 --> 00:05:52,423
Neonatal iron stores are related to maternal iron status and to timing of cord clamping.

48
00:05:52,423 --> 00:05:58,336
Iron deficiency often manifests as an appreciable drop in hemoglobin concentration.

49
00:05:58,336 --> 00:06:04,061
Classic morphological evidence is erythrocyte hypochromia and microcytosis .

50
00:06:04,061 --> 00:06:07,386
These may be less prominent in the pregnant woman.

51
00:06:07,386 --> 00:06:14,673
A mean corpuscular volume <80 fL is classically seen (Appendix, p. 1227) .

52
00:06:14,673 --> 00:06:24,148
Serum ferritin levels normally decline during pregnancy, and levels below 10 to 15 mg/L confirm iron-deficiency anemia.

53
00:06:24,148 --> 00:06:27,898
In addition, hepcidin levels drop in pregnancy.

54
00:06:27,898 --> 00:06:33,673
This hormone inhibits iron transport by binding to the iron-export channel ferroportin.

55
00:06:33,673 --> 00:06:36,261
Figure 59-1.

56
00:06:36,261 --> 00:06:45,023
In this peripheral blood smear, iron-deficiency anemia is reflected by scattered microcytic and hypochromic red cells (arrows).

57
00:06:45,023 --> 00:06:49,036
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Siayareh Rambally.

58
00:06:49,036 --> 00:06:59,111
Routinely in pregnancy, daily oral supplementation with 30 to 60 mg of elemental iron and 400 μg of folic acid is recommended .

59
00:06:59,111 --> 00:07:10,798
For iron-deficiency anemia, resolution and restitution of iron stores can be accomplished with simple iron salts that provide approximately 200 mg daily of elemental iron.

60
00:07:10,798 --> 00:07:15,073
These include ferrous sulfate, fumarate, or gluconate.

61
00:07:15,073 --> 00:07:20,536
If a woman cannot take oral iron preparations, parenteral therapy is given.

62
00:07:20,536 --> 00:07:29,761
Although both are administered intravenously, ferrous sucrose is safer than iron dextran (Auerbach, 2020; Pavord, 2020).

63
00:07:29,761 --> 00:07:38,673
Moderate iron-deficiency anemia responds to adequate iron therapy, and the hematological response is an elevated reticulocyte count.

64
00:07:38,673 --> 00:07:48,573
In gravidas, the rise of hemoglobin concentration or hematocrit is typically slower than in nonpregnant women due to the larger plasma volumes of pregnancy.

65
00:07:48,573 --> 00:08:02,248
Hemoglobin and ferritin levels show equivalent rises in women treated with either oral or parenteral iron therapy (Breymann, 2017; Daru, 2016; Neogi, 2019).

66
00:08:02,248 --> 00:08:05,486
# Anemia from Acute Blood Loss.

67
00:08:05,486 --> 00:08:13,986
In early pregnancy, anemia caused by acute blood loss is common with abortion, ectopic pregnancy, and hydatidiform mole.

68
00:08:13,986 --> 00:08:18,598
Postpartum, anemia commonly stems from obstetrical hemorrhage.

69
00:08:18,598 --> 00:08:25,823
Massive hemorrhage demands immediate treatment and is described in Chapter 44 (p. 771).

70
00:08:25,823 --> 00:08:40,898
If a moderately anemic woman—defined by a hemoglobin value of approximately 7 g/dL—is hemodynamically stable, is able to ambulate without adverse symptoms, and is not septic, then blood transfusions are not indicated.

71
00:08:40,898 --> 00:08:45,711
Instead, oral iron therapy is provided for at least 3 months.

72
00:08:45,711 --> 00:08:48,736
# Anemia of Chronic Disease.

73
00:08:48,736 --> 00:08:54,311
Diseases associated with chronic inflammation can cause mild to moderate anemia.

74
00:08:54,311 --> 00:08:59,398
Anemia stems from several cytokines produced by inflammatory cells.

75
00:08:59,398 --> 00:09:03,873
These restrict erythropoiesis and shorten red cell lifespan.

76
00:09:03,873 --> 00:09:11,373
Hepcidin levels are elevated with chronic inflammation, and this inhibits ironexporting activity from enterocytes .

77
00:09:11,373 --> 00:09:21,323
Anemia is usually characterized by slightly hypochromic and microcytic erythrocytes, low transferrin saturation, and a high serum ferritin level .

78
00:09:21,323 --> 00:09:26,673
Along with iron deficiency, it is the most common form of anemia worldwide.

79
00:09:26,673 --> 00:09:32,236
During pregnancy, women with chronic disorders may develop anemia for the first time.

80
00:09:32,236 --> 00:09:38,036
In those with preexisting anemia, it may worsen as plasma volume expands.

81
00:09:38,036 --> 00:09:45,686
Frequent causes include chronic renal insufficiency, inflammatory bowel disease, and connective tissue disorders.

82
00:09:45,686 --> 00:09:48,948
Others are granulomatous infections and

83
00:09:49,108 --> 00:09:51,458
malignant neoplasms.

84
00:09:51,408 --> 00:09:58,883
Chronic renal insufficiency is the most common disorder that we have encountered during pregnancy as a cause of this anemia type.

85
00:09:58,883 --> 00:10:03,233
Some cases are accompanied by erythropoietin deficiency.

86
00:10:03,233 --> 00:10:11,945
During pregnancy in women with mild chronic renal disease, the degree of red cell mass expansion is inversely related to renal impairment .

87
00:10:11,945 --> 00:10:19,295
At the same time, plasma volume expansion usually is normal, and thus anemia severity intensifies .

88
00:10:19,295 --> 00:10:23,283
For treatment, adequate iron stores must be ensured.

89
00:10:23,283 --> 00:10:31,570
Recombinant erythropoietin, with or without intravenous iron, has been used to treat anemia stemming from chronic disease .

90
00:10:31,570 --> 00:10:41,358
In pregnancies complicated by chronic renal insufficiency, recombinant erythropoietin is usually considered when the hematocrit approximates 20 percent .

91
00:10:41,358 --> 00:10:47,295
One side effect is hypertension, which is already prevalent in women with renal disease.

92
00:10:47,295 --> 00:10:52,783
Red cell aplasia and antierythropoietin antibodies also have been reported .

93
00:10:52,783 --> 00:10:55,758
# Megaloblastic Anemia.

94
00:10:55,758 --> 00:11:04,595
Folic Acid Deficiency Megaloblastic anemias are characterized by blood and bone marrow abnormalities from impaired DNA synthesis.

95
00:11:04,595 --> 00:11:11,708
This leads to large cells with arrested nuclear maturation, whereas the cytoplasm matures more normally.

96
00:11:11,708 --> 00:11:18,470
Worldwide, the pregnancy prevalence of this anemia varies considerably but is low in the United States.

97
00:11:18,470 --> 00:11:25,333
Megaloblastic anemia developing during pregnancy almost always results from folic acid deficiency.

98
00:11:25,333 --> 00:11:30,170
In the past, this condition was called pernicious anemia of pregnancy.

99
00:11:30,170 --> 00:11:37,533
It usually is found in women who do not consume fresh green leafy vegetables, legumes, or animal protein.

100
00:11:37,533 --> 00:11:45,845
As folate deficiency and anemia worsen, anorexia often intensifies and further aggravates the dietary deficiency.

101
00:11:45,845 --> 00:11:58,658
Other causes are malabsorption syndromes that include tropical sprue, extensive jejunal resection, gastrectomy, and Crohn disease; hemolytic anemias; malignancy; and some antifolate drugs .

102
00:11:58,658 --> 00:12:03,895
Nonpregnant women need 50 to 100 μg of folic acid daily.

103
00:12:03,895 --> 00:12:10,208
During pregnancy, requirements rise, and 400 μg/d is recommended.

104
00:12:10,208 --> 00:12:18,120
The earliest biochemical evidence is low plasma folic acid concentrations (Appendix, p. 1227).

105
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:25,108
Early morphological changes usually include hypersegmented neutrophils and macrocytic erythrocytes.

106
00:12:25,108 --> 00:12:34,620
As the anemia worsens, peripheral nucleated erythrocytes appear, and bone marrow examination discloses megaloblastic erythropoiesis.

107
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:41,133
Anemia may then become severe, and thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, or both may develop.

108
00:12:41,133 --> 00:12:50,083
The fetus and placenta effectively extract folate from maternal circulation, and the fetus is not anemic despite severe maternal anemia.

109
00:12:50,083 --> 00:12:58,195
For treatment, 5 to 15 mg of oral folic acid is provided with iron, and a nutritious diet is encouraged .

110
00:12:58,195 --> 00:13:05,808
At 4 to 7 days of treatment, the reticulocyte count is higher, and leukopenia and thrombocytopenia are corrected.

111
00:13:05,808 --> 00:13:10,845
For anemia prevention, a diet should contain sufficient folic acid.

112
00:13:10,845 --> 00:13:17,158
Moreover, the role of folate deficiency in the genesis of neural-tube defects is well studied .

113
00:13:17,158 --> 00:13:28,370
Vitamin B12 Deficiency With pregnancy, vitamin B12 levels are lower than nonpregnant values because levels of binding proteins, namely, the transcobalamins decline.

114
00:13:28,370 --> 00:13:35,195
However, in most gravidas, megaloblastic anemia is rarely from vitamin B12 deficiency.

115
00:13:35,195 --> 00:13:43,370
Of predisposing conditions, vitamin B12 deficiency in pregnancy is more likely encountered following gastric resection.

116
00:13:43,370 --> 00:13:52,495
Women who have undergone total gastrectomy require 1000 μg of intramuscular vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin) monthly.

117
00:13:52,495 --> 00:14:02,858
Those with a partial gastrectomy usually do not need supplementation, but adequate serum vitamin B12 levels should be ensured (Appendix, p. 1230).

118
00:14:02,858 --> 00:14:13,645
Other causes of megaloblastic anemia from vitamin B12 deficiency include Crohn disease, ileal resection, some drugs, and bacterial overgrowth in the small bowel .

119
00:14:13,645 --> 00:14:21,633
Addisonian pernicious anemia results from absent intrinsic factor that is requisite for dietary vitamin B12 absorption.

120
00:14:21,633 --> 00:14:29,945
This autoimmune disorder usually has its onset after age 40 years, which accounts for its uncommon occurrence in pregnancy .

121
00:14:29,945 --> 00:14:32,645
# Hemolytic Anemia.

122
00:14:32,645 --> 00:14:36,595
Several conditions accelerate erythrocyte destruction.

123
00:14:36,595 --> 00:14:39,283
Hemolysis may be a primary

124
00:14:39,436 --> 00:14:45,098
disorder, and sickle-cell disease and hereditary spherocytosis are examples.

125
00:14:45,048 --> 00:14:53,036
In others, hemolysis complicates an underlying condition such as systemic lupus erythematosus or preeclampsia.

126
00:14:53,036 --> 00:14:58,836
Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia from malignancy has been reported in pregnancy .

127
00:14:58,836 --> 00:15:04,073
Autoimmune Hemolysis The cause of aberrant antibody production is unknown.

128
00:15:04,073 --> 00:15:10,161
Typically, both the direct and indirect antiglobulin (Coombs) tests are positive.

129
00:15:10,161 --> 00:15:19,861
Anemias caused by these factors may be due to warm-active autoantibodies (80 to 90 percent), cold-active antibodies, or a combination.

130
00:15:19,861 --> 00:15:28,398
These syndromes may also be classified as primary (idiopathic) or secondary due to underlying diseases or other factors.

131
00:15:28,398 --> 00:15:39,661
Examples of the secondary group include lymphomas and leukemias, connective tissue diseases, infections, chronic inflammatory diseases, and drug-induced antibodies .

132
00:15:39,661 --> 00:15:48,448
Coldagglutinin disease may be induced by infectious etiologies such as Mycoplasma pneumoniae or Epstein-Barr viral mononucleosis.

133
00:15:48,448 --> 00:16:00,611
Hemolysis and positive antiglobulin test results may be the consequence of either immunoglobulin M (IgM) or immunoglobulin G (IgG) antierythrocyte antibodies.

134
00:16:00,611 --> 00:16:05,336
When thrombocytopenia is comorbid, it is termed Evans syndrome .

135
00:16:05,336 --> 00:16:09,598
In pregnancy, hemolysis can be markedly accelerated.

136
00:16:09,598 --> 00:16:18,273
Low-dose rituximab (Truxima)—100mg weekly for 4 weeks—coupled with prednisone, is first-line treatment .

137
00:16:18,273 --> 00:16:22,823
Coincidental thrombocytopenia usually corrects with therapy.

138
00:16:22,823 --> 00:16:33,336
Transfusion of red cells is complicated by antierythrocyte antibodies, but warming the donor cells to body temperature may decrease their destruction by cold agglutinins.

139
00:16:33,336 --> 00:16:36,911
In rare cases, the fetus may be involved .

140
00:16:36,911 --> 00:16:44,323
Drug-induced Hemolysis These hemolytic anemias must be differentiated from other causes of autoimmune hemolysis.

141
00:16:44,323 --> 00:16:49,248
In most cases, hemolysis is mild and resolves with drug withdrawal.

142
00:16:49,248 --> 00:16:52,798
Subsequently, avoiding the drug is preventive.

143
00:16:52,798 --> 00:16:58,998
One mechanism is hemolysis induced through drug-mediated immunological injury to red cells.

144
00:16:58,998 --> 00:17:06,298
If bound to a red cell protein, the drug may act as a highaffinity hapten to which antidrug antibodies attach.

145
00:17:06,298 --> 00:17:11,748
An example is IgM antipenicillin or anticephalosporin antibodies.

146
00:17:11,748 --> 00:17:23,023
Garratty and colleagues (1999) described seven women with severe Coombs-positive hemolysis stimulated by cefotetan given as prophylaxis for obstetrical procedures.

147
00:17:23,023 --> 00:17:26,611
Alpha-methyldopa can cause similar hemolysis .

148
00:17:26,611 --> 00:17:35,536
Some other drugs, which include probenecid, quinidine, and rifampin, act as lowaffinity haptens and adhere to cell membrane proteins.

149
00:17:35,536 --> 00:17:47,586
A frequent mechanism for drug-induced hemolysis relates to a congenital erythrocyte enzymatic defect, such as glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency (p. 1052).

150
00:17:47,586 --> 00:17:56,811
Pregnancy-associated Hemolysis Unexplained severe hemolytic anemia can develop during early pregnancy and resolves within months postpartum.

151
00:17:56,811 --> 00:18:01,648
Clear immune mechanisms or red cell defects are not contributory.

152
00:18:01,648 --> 00:18:08,973
Because the fetus–neonate also may demonstrate transient hemolysis, an immunological cause is suspected.

153
00:18:08,973 --> 00:18:14,061
Maternal corticosteroid treatment is often—but not always—effective .

154
00:18:14,061 --> 00:18:21,398
We have cared for a woman who during each pregnancy developed severe hemolysis with anemia that responded to prednisone.

155
00:18:21,398 --> 00:18:27,223
Her fetuses were not affected, and hemolysis abated spontaneously after delivery.

156
00:18:27,223 --> 00:18:32,573
In some cases, hemolysis is induced by conditions unique to pregnancy.

157
00:18:32,573 --> 00:18:44,961
Mild microangiopathic hemolysis with thrombocytopenia is relatively common with severe preeclampsia and eclampsia (Cunningham, 2015; Kenny, 2015).

158
00:18:44,961 --> 00:18:54,973
This HELLP (hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, low platelet count) syndrome is discussed in Chapter 40 (p. 699).

159
00:18:54,973 --> 00:19:01,948
Another example is acute fatty liver of pregnancy, which causes moderate to severe hemolytic anemia .

160
00:19:01,948 --> 00:19:15,673
Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria Although commonly regarded as a hemolytic anemia, this hemopoietic stem cell disorder is characterized by formation of defective platelets, granulocytes, and erythrocytes.

161
00:19:15,673 --> 00:19:23,748
Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria is acquired and arises from one abnormal clone of cells, much like a neoplasm.

162
00:19:23,748 --> 00:19:30,061
In contrast, one mutated X-linked gene responsible for this condition is termed PIG-A

163
00:19:30,220 --> 00:19:42,920
because it codes for phosphatidylinositol glycan protein A. Resultant abnormal anchor proteins of the erythrocyte and granulocyte membrane make these cells unusually susceptible to lysis by complement.

164
00:19:42,870 --> 00:19:49,395
A serious complication is thrombosis, which is heightened in the hypercoagulable state of pregnancy.

165
00:19:49,395 --> 00:19:55,532
Chronic hemolysis has an insidious onset, and its severity ranges from mild to lethal.

166
00:19:55,532 --> 00:20:01,107
Hemoglobinuria develops at irregular intervals and is not necessarily nocturnal.

167
00:20:01,107 --> 00:20:06,607
Hemolysis may be initiated by transfusions, infections, or surgery.

168
00:20:06,607 --> 00:20:16,920
Almost 40 percent of patients suffer venous thromboembolism (VTE) and may also experience renal failure, hypertension, and Budd-Chiari syndrome.

169
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:22,532
To counter the VTE risk, prophylactic anticoagulation is recommended .

170
00:20:22,532 --> 00:20:33,720
The preferred treatment is eculizumab (Soliris), an antibody that inhibits complement activation (Kelly, 2015; Stefanovic, 2019).

171
00:20:33,720 --> 00:20:36,882
The drug is apparently safe in pregnancy .

172
00:20:36,882 --> 00:20:45,007
Median survival after diagnosis of the syndrome is 10 years, and bone marrow transplantation is the definitive treatment.

173
00:20:45,007 --> 00:20:51,295
During pregnancy, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria can be serious and unpredictable.

174
00:20:51,295 --> 00:21:01,520
Complications may affect up to three fourths of affected women, and the maternal mortality rate in the past was 10 to 20 percent (de Guibert, 2011).

175
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:07,770
Complications more often develop postpartum, and half of affected women develop VTE.

176
00:21:07,770 --> 00:21:16,457
Kelly and coworkers (2015) described 75 pregnancies in 61 affected women treated with eculizumab.

177
00:21:16,457 --> 00:21:21,382
They described no maternal deaths, but the stillbirth rate was 4 percent.

178
00:21:21,382 --> 00:21:26,707
Miyasaka and associates (2016) found similar results.

179
00:21:26,707 --> 00:21:38,270
Bacterial Toxins The most fulminant acquired hemolytic anemia encountered during pregnancy is caused by the exotoxin of Clostridium perfringens or by group A βhemolytic streptococcus .

180
00:21:38,270 --> 00:21:48,020
In additional, endotoxin of gramnegative bacteria, that is, lipopolysaccharide, may be accompanied by hemolysis and mild to moderate anemia .

181
00:21:48,020 --> 00:21:52,920
For example, anemia often accompanies acute pyelonephritis.

182
00:21:52,920 --> 00:22:03,995
With normal erythropoietin production, red cell mass is restored following infection resolution (Cavenee, 1994; Dotters-Katz, 2013).

183
00:22:03,995 --> 00:22:13,520
Inherited Erythrocyte Membrane Defects The normal erythrocyte is a flexible biconcave disc that allows numerous cycles of reversible deformations.

184
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:19,995
Several genes encode erythrocyte structural membrane proteins and intraerythrocytic enzymes.

185
00:22:19,995 --> 00:22:24,145
Various mutations can destabilize the lipid bilayer.

186
00:22:24,145 --> 00:22:32,632
The loss of lipids from the cell membrane causes a surface area deficiency and poorly deformable erythrocytes that undergo hemolysis .

187
00:22:32,632 --> 00:22:36,457
Anemia severity depends on the degree of rigidity.

188
00:22:36,457 --> 00:22:45,157
Erythrocyte morphology similarly is dependent on these factors, and these disorders are usually named after the most dominant red cell shape.

189
00:22:45,157 --> 00:22:52,182
Three examples are hereditary spherocytosis, pyropoikilocytosis, and ovalocytosis.

190
00:22:52,182 --> 00:22:59,607
Hemolytic anemias that compose this group of inherited membrane defects are among the most common complicating pregnancy.

191
00:22:59,607 --> 00:23:06,682
Mutations are usually an autosomally dominant, variably penetrant α- and β-spectrin deficiency.

192
00:23:06,682 --> 00:23:21,445
Others are dominant or recessive gene mutations that result from deficiency of ankyrin, band 3, 4.1, and 4.2, or combinations of these (Luzzato, 2018; Rencic, 2017).

193
00:23:21,445 --> 00:23:32,195
The degrees of anemia and jaundice vary, and diagnosis is confirmed by identification of abnormal erythrocytes on peripheral smear and their increased osmotic fragility.

194
00:23:32,195 --> 00:23:42,570
Spherocytic anemias may be associated with a crisis typified by severe anemia from accelerated hemolysis, and it develops in patients with an enlarged spleen.

195
00:23:42,570 --> 00:23:48,782
Infection can also accelerate hemolysis or suppress erythropoiesis to worsen anemia.

196
00:23:48,782 --> 00:23:53,157
An example of the latter is parvovirus B19 infection .

197
00:23:53,157 --> 00:23:59,107
In severe cases, splenectomy reduces hemolysis, anemia, and jaundice.

198
00:23:59,107 --> 00:24:05,195
In general, women with inherited erythrocyte membrane defects do well during pregnancy.

199
00:24:05,195 --> 00:24:11,657
Oral folic acid supplementation of 4 mg daily is given to sustain erythropoiesis.

200
00:24:11,657 --> 00:24:21,395
Women with hereditary spherocytosis cared for at Parkland Hospital had hematocrits ranging from 23 to 41 percent, with a mean of 31 .

201
00:24:21,395 --> 00:24:24,995
Reticulocyte counts varied from 1 to 23

202
00:24:25,156 --> 00:24:26,693
percent.

203
00:24:26,643 --> 00:24:31,381
Among 50 pregnancies in 23 women, eight women miscarried.

204
00:24:31,381 --> 00:24:36,556
Four of 42 infants were born preterm, but none was growth restricted.

205
00:24:36,556 --> 00:24:42,893
Infection in four women intensified hemolysis, and three of these required transfusions.

206
00:24:42,893 --> 00:24:48,031
Similar results were reported by Pajor and colleagues (1993).

207
00:24:48,031 --> 00:24:52,531
Because these disorders are inherited, the newborn may be affected.

208
00:24:52,531 --> 00:25:00,931
Preconceptional counseling emphasizes folic acid supplementation and provides an opportunity to discuss prenatal diagnosis.

209
00:25:00,931 --> 00:25:11,031
Celkan and Alhaj (2008) report prenatal diagnosis via cordocentesis at 18 weeks’ gestation and testing for osmotic fragility.

210
00:25:11,031 --> 00:25:20,743
Although fetal anemia is uncommon, newborns with hereditary spherocytosis may manifest hyperbilirubinemia and anemia shortly after birth.

211
00:25:20,743 --> 00:25:31,481
Erythrocyte Enzyme Deficiencies An intraerythrocytic deficiency of enzymes that permit anaerobic glucose metabolism may cause hereditary nonspherocytic anemia.

212
00:25:31,481 --> 00:25:35,518
Most of these mutations are autosomal recessive traits.

213
00:25:35,518 --> 00:25:42,018
Most episodes of severe anemia with these enzyme deficiencies are induced by drugs or infections.

214
00:25:42,018 --> 00:25:50,881
Pyruvate kinase deficiency has a frequency of 1:10,000 persons and is associated with variable degrees of anemia .

215
00:25:50,881 --> 00:25:59,718
Recurrent transfusions in homozygous carriers can lead to iron overload, and associated myocardial dysfunction should be monitored .

216
00:25:59,718 --> 00:26:06,581
The fetus that is homozygous for this mutation may develop hydrops fetalis from anemia and heart failure .

217
00:26:06,581 --> 00:26:15,456
Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency is complex because there are more than 200 enzyme variants.

218
00:26:15,456 --> 00:26:27,468
The most common stem from a base substitution that leads to an amino acid replacement and a broad range of phenotypic severity (Luzzatto, 2018; Puig, 2013).

219
00:26:27,468 --> 00:26:36,681
In the homozygous or A variant, both X chromosomes are affected, and erythrocytes are markedly deficient in G6PD activity.

220
00:26:36,681 --> 00:26:44,968
Approximately 2 percent of AfricanAmerican women are affected, and the heterozygous variant is found in 10 to 15 percent.

221
00:26:44,968 --> 00:26:52,606
In both cases, random X-chromosome inactivation—lyonization —results in variable enzyme activity.

222
00:26:52,606 --> 00:27:01,443
During pregnancy, hemolysis severity in G6PD-deficient heterozygotes or homozygotes is related to enzyme activity.

223
00:27:01,443 --> 00:27:08,156
Anemia is usually episodic, but some variants induce chronic nonspherocytic hemolysis.

224
00:27:08,156 --> 00:27:16,818
Because young erythrocytes contain more enzyme activity, anemia stabilizes and corrects soon after the inciting cause is resolved.

225
00:27:16,818 --> 00:27:32,056
These women are offered preconceptional counseling to discuss risks of adverse pregnancy outcomes, precipitating factors of hemolysis such as pyelonephritis and pneumonia, and the limited list of safe medications available for treatment during pregnancy.

226
00:27:32,056 --> 00:27:41,318
For example, macrodantin is used for pyelonephritis suppression in pregnancy but can induce hemolysis in those with G6PD deficiency.

227
00:27:41,318 --> 00:27:52,231
Because fetal and neonatal manifestations are possible, these patients should undergo genetic counseling, with possible partner testing, to assess fetal transmission risk.

228
00:27:52,231 --> 00:27:55,843
# Aplastic and Hypoplastic Anemia.

229
00:27:55,843 --> 00:28:03,606
Aplastic anemia is a grave complication characterized by pancytopenia and markedly hypocellular bone marrow .

230
00:28:03,606 --> 00:28:09,018
The functional defect is a marked decline in the number of committed marrow stem cells.

231
00:28:09,018 --> 00:28:15,931
Etiologies are multiple, and the inciting cause can be identified in approximately a third of cases.

232
00:28:15,931 --> 00:28:28,068
These include immunological disorders, drugs, other chemicals, infection, irradiation, leukemia, and inherited conditions such as Fanconi anemia and Diamond-Blackfan syndrome .

233
00:28:28,068 --> 00:28:39,331
Immunosuppressive therapy is given, and in some nonresponders, eltrombopag (Promacta) is successful (Townsley, 2017; Young, 2018).

234
00:28:39,331 --> 00:28:47,993
Definitive treatment is bone marrow transplantation, and approximately three fourths of patients have a good response and long-term survival .

235
00:28:47,993 --> 00:28:57,906
Umbilical cord blood–derived stem cells also can serve as a potential transplant source (Moise, 2005; Pinto, 2008).

236
00:28:57,906 --> 00:29:03,468
Pregnancy Hypoplastic or aplastic anemia complicating pregnancy is rare.

237
00:29:03,468 --> 00:29:10,543
A study of 60 pregnancies complicated by aplastic anemia found that half were diagnosed during pregnancy .

238
00:29:10,543 --> 00:29:17,318
In another series of 19 pregnancies, the complication rate was 79 percent, but no women

239
00:29:17,476 --> 00:29:18,926
died .

240
00:29:18,876 --> 00:29:32,451
Pregnancy-induced hypoplastic anemia is rare, and the anemia and other cytopenias improve or remit following delivery or pregnancy termination (Choudhry, 2002; Edahiro, 2020).

241
00:29:32,451 --> 00:29:37,063
In some cases, anemia recurs in a subsequent pregnancy.

242
00:29:37,063 --> 00:29:42,251
Diamond-Blackfan anemia is rare form of pure red cell hypoplasia.

243
00:29:42,251 --> 00:29:48,413
Approximately 40 percent of cases are familial and have autosomal dominant inheritance .

244
00:29:48,413 --> 00:29:52,463
The response to glucocorticoid therapy is usually good.

245
00:29:52,463 --> 00:29:58,726
Continuous treatment is necessary, and most become at least partially transfusion dependent .

246
00:29:58,726 --> 00:30:13,313
In a study of 64 pregnancies complicated by this syndrome, two thirds had problems related to placental vascular etiologies that included miscarriage, preeclampsia, preterm birth, fetal-growth restriction, or stillbirth .

247
00:30:13,313 --> 00:30:22,088
Gaucher disease is an autosomally recessive lysosomal enzyme deficiency characterized by deficient activity of acid β-glucosidase.

248
00:30:22,088 --> 00:30:27,826
Affected women have anemia and thrombocytopenia that usually worsens in pregnancy.

249
00:30:27,826 --> 00:30:36,476
Elstein and colleagues (1997) described six pregnant women whose disease improved with alglucerase enzyme replacement.

250
00:30:36,476 --> 00:30:42,076
Imiglucerase (Cerezyme) is human recombinant enzyme replacement therapy.

251
00:30:42,076 --> 00:30:46,963
The major risks with hypoplastic anemia are hemorrhage and infection.

252
00:30:46,963 --> 00:30:53,513
Rates of preterm labor, preeclampsia, fetal-growth restriction, and stillbirth are increased .

253
00:30:53,513 --> 00:31:02,513
Management depends on gestational age, and supportive care includes continuous infection surveillance and prompt antimicrobial therapy.

254
00:31:02,513 --> 00:31:06,976
Granulocyte transfusions are given only during infections.

255
00:31:06,976 --> 00:31:14,963
Red cells are transfused to improve symptomatic anemia and routinely to maintain the hematocrit above 20 volumes percent.

256
00:31:14,963 --> 00:31:18,951
Platelet transfusions may be needed to control hemorrhage.

257
00:31:18,951 --> 00:31:26,163
Mortality rates reported since 1960 have averaged nearly 50 percent but have declined more recently .

258
00:31:26,163 --> 00:31:34,476
Bone Marrow Transplantation Several reports describe successful pregnancies in women who have undergone bone marrow transplantation.

259
00:31:34,476 --> 00:31:42,676
In their review, Sanders and coworkers (1996) reported 72 pregnancies in 41 such women.

260
00:31:42,676 --> 00:31:50,826
In the 52 pregnancies resulting in a liveborn neonate, almost half were complicated by preterm delivery or hypertension.

261
00:31:50,826 --> 00:32:01,188
Data from the National Cancer Institute regarding 102 pregnancies following transplantation showed 71 liveborns, of which 16 were preterm.

262
00:32:01,188 --> 00:32:07,451
Of all women, 20 percent had decreased hemopoiesis, and transfusions were required .

263
00:32:07,451 --> 00:32:14,551
Carter and associates (2006) reported decreased fertility but generally good pregnancy outcomes.

264
00:32:14,551 --> 00:32:23,338
Our experiences with a few of these women indicate that they have normal pregnancy-augmented erythropoiesis and total blood volume expansion.

265
00:32:23,338 --> 00:32:29,863
In pregnancy, cell-free DNA study results must account for donor DNA contributions .

266
00:32:29,863 --> 00:32:32,026
Polycythemias.

267
00:32:32,026 --> 00:32:38,663
Excessive erythrocytosis during pregnancy is usually secondary and related to chronic hypoxia.

268
00:32:38,663 --> 00:32:45,438
Etiologies include maternal congenital cyanotic cardiac disease or a chronic pulmonary disorder.

269
00:32:45,438 --> 00:32:56,838
In addition, we have encountered otherwise healthy pregnant women who were heavy smokers, had chronic bronchitis, and had hematocrits ranging from 55 to 60 volumes percent!

270
00:32:56,838 --> 00:33:02,913
If polycythemia is severe, the probability of a successful pregnancy outcome is low.

271
00:33:02,913 --> 00:33:15,713
Polycythemia vera is a primary clonal myeloproliferative hemopoietic stem cell disorder characterized by excessive proliferation of erythroid, myeloid, and megakaryocytic precursors.

272
00:33:15,713 --> 00:33:20,063
Virtually all patients have a JAK2 gene mutation .

273
00:33:20,063 --> 00:33:25,988
Symptoms are related to greater blood viscosity, and thrombotic complications are common.

274
00:33:25,988 --> 00:33:31,088
Treatment of nonpregnant patients is with hydroxyurea or ruxolitinib.

275
00:33:31,088 --> 00:33:42,801
Fetal loss rates are high in women with polycythemia vera, and pregnancy outcome may be improved with aspirin therapy (Bertozzi, 2018; Dewarrat, 2020).

276
00:33:42,801 --> 00:33:48,526
Women with a history of VTE are given prophylaxis with low-molecular-weight heparin .

277
00:33:48,526 --> 00:33:54,276
If cytoreduction is required during pregnancy, interferon alpha may be considered .

278
00:33:54,276 --> 00:33:56,563
Hemoglobinopathies.

279
00:33:56,563 --> 00:34:00,013
# Sickle-cell Hemoglobinopathies.

280
00:34:00,013 --> 00:34:08,476
Pathophysiology Hemoglobin A is the most common hemoglobin tetramer and consists of two α- and two β-globin chains.

281
00:34:08,476 --> 00:34:12,626
Genes HBA1 and HBA2 each code for

282
00:34:12,796 --> 00:34:17,233
α-globin, whereas only HBB codes for β-globin.

283
00:34:17,183 --> 00:34:27,571
Sickle hemoglobin (hemoglobin S) originates from a single β-globin substitution of glutamic acid by valine, which stems from an A-for-T substitution.

284
00:34:27,571 --> 00:34:36,458
Hemoglobin C originates from a single β-globin substitution of glutamic acid by lysine, which stems from a Tfor-C substitution.

285
00:34:36,458 --> 00:34:57,221
Hemoglobinopathies that can result in clinical features of the sickle-cell syndrome include sickle-cell anemia (Hb SS); sickle-cell hemoglobin C disease (Hb SC); sickle-cell β-thalassemia disease (either Hb S/B0 or Hb S/B+); and sickle-cell E disease (Hb SE).

286
00:34:57,221 --> 00:35:00,871
All are associated with higher pregnancy morbidity.

287
00:35:00,871 --> 00:35:08,121
When deoxygenated, red cells containing hemoglobin S undergo sickling, and the hemoglobin aggregates .

288
00:35:08,121 --> 00:35:15,096
Constant sickling and unsickling damages the cell membrane, and its shape may become irreversibly sickled.

289
00:35:15,096 --> 00:35:25,696
Events that slow erythrocyte transit through the microcirculation include adhesion to endothelial cells, erythrocyte dehydration, and vasomotor dysregulation.

290
00:35:25,696 --> 00:35:28,296
Figure 59-2.

291
00:35:28,296 --> 00:35:34,071
Peripheral smear of sickle cells (arrows) characteristic of sickle-cell anemia.

292
00:35:34,071 --> 00:35:37,796
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Imran Hitto.

293
00:35:37,796 --> 00:35:43,846
Clinically, sickling episodes are marked by ischemia and infarction in various organs.

294
00:35:43,846 --> 00:35:48,308
The sickle-cell crisis produces pain, which is often severe.

295
00:35:48,308 --> 00:35:54,096
Aplastic, megaloblastic, sequestration, and hemolytic crises can develop.

296
00:35:54,096 --> 00:36:22,108
Chronic and acute changes from sickling include bony abnormalities such as osteonecrosis of femoral and humeral heads, renal medullary damage, autosplenectomy in homozygous SS patients but splenomegaly in other variants, hepatomegaly, ventricular hypertrophy, pulmonary infarctions, cerebrovascular accidents, leg ulcers, and a propensity for infection and sepsis (Benz, 2018; Ware, 2017).

297
00:36:22,108 --> 00:36:28,308
Pulmonary hypertension can develop and is found in 20 percent of adults with SS hemoglobin .

298
00:36:28,308 --> 00:36:33,896
Other sequelae are cerebrovascular aneurysms and sickle-cell vasculopathy .

299
00:36:33,896 --> 00:36:38,146
Treatment Good supportive care is essential to prevent mortality.

300
00:36:38,146 --> 00:36:42,833
Specific therapies are evolving, and many are still experimental.

301
00:36:42,833 --> 00:36:48,733
One treatment is hemoglobin F induction with drugs that stimulate γ-globin synthesis.

302
00:36:48,733 --> 00:36:52,746
Remember that hemoglobin F is prominent in fetal life .

303
00:36:52,746 --> 00:36:58,971
This induction raises hemoglobin F levels, which inhibits hemoglobin S polymerization.

304
00:36:58,971 --> 00:37:06,671
One agent is hydroxyurea, which augments hemoglobin F production and reduces the number of sickling episodes .

305
00:37:06,671 --> 00:37:10,396
Hydroxyurea is teratogenic in animals.

306
00:37:10,396 --> 00:37:20,108
A preliminary 17-year surveillance of antenatally exposed children was reassuring (Ballas, 2009; Briggs, 2017).

307
00:37:20,108 --> 00:37:31,346
Other agents include biologic antibody drugs and l-glutamine (Ataga, 2017, Niihara, 2018; Stefanovic, 2019).

308
00:37:31,346 --> 00:37:43,821
Various forms of hemopoietic cell transplantation are emerging as “cures” for sickle-cell syndromes and severe thalassemias (Oringanje, 2013; Kanter, 2021).

309
00:37:43,821 --> 00:37:55,608
Last, gene therapy has been accomplished by viral vector–mediated addition of a β-globin gene into stem cells (Harrison, 2019; Ribeil, 2017).

310
00:37:55,608 --> 00:37:59,196
# Sickle-cell Syndromes During Pregnancy.

311
00:37:59,196 --> 00:38:10,946
Pregnancy is a serious burden to women with any major sickle hemoglobinopathy, particularly those with hemoglobin SS (Kuo, 2016; Villers, 2008).

312
00:38:10,946 --> 00:38:21,733
Maternal mortality rates have improved, but perinatal morbidity and mortality rates remain formidable (Bae, 2021; Oteng-Ntim, 2015).

313
00:38:21,733 --> 00:38:27,746
Thus, women with sickle-cell hemoglobinopathies require close prenatal observation.

314
00:38:27,746 --> 00:38:35,383
Specifically, any factor that impairs erythropoiesis or increases red cell destruction aggravates the anemia.

315
00:38:35,383 --> 00:38:42,896
Prenatal folic acid supplementation with 4 mg daily is needed to support rapid red blood cell turnover.

316
00:38:42,896 --> 00:38:45,508
Table 59-3.

317
00:38:45,508 --> 00:38:55,996
Pregnancy Morbidity with Hemoglobin SS and SC Disease One danger is that a symptomatic woman may categorically be considered to be suffering from a sickle-cell crisis.

318
00:38:55,996 --> 00:39:03,458
As a result, serious obstetrical or medical problems that cause pain, anemia, or both may be overlooked.

319
00:39:03,458 --> 00:39:09,933
Examples are ectopic pregnancy, placental abruption, pyelonephritis, or appendicitis.

320
00:39:09,933 --> 00:39:17,371
Thus, a diagnosis of sickle-cell crisis should be applied only after all other possible causes have been excluded.

321
00:39:17,371 --> 00:39:18,971
Pain with

322
00:39:19,132 --> 00:39:28,244
sickle-cell syndromes is caused by intense sequestration of sickled erythrocytes and infarction in various organs, particularly bone marrow.

323
00:39:28,194 --> 00:39:36,407
These episodes develop acutely, especially late in pregnancy, during labor and delivery, and early in the puerperium.

324
00:39:36,407 --> 00:39:41,094
No randomized trials have evaluated treatment during pregnancy.

325
00:39:41,094 --> 00:39:47,644
At minimum, intravenous fluids are given, and opioids are administered promptly for severe pain.

326
00:39:47,644 --> 00:39:53,257
Oxygen via nasal cannula may decrease the sickling intensity at the capillary level.

327
00:39:53,257 --> 00:40:02,344
We have found that red cell transfusions after the onset of severe pain do not dramatically relieve pain intensity and may not shorten its duration.

328
00:40:02,344 --> 00:40:11,169
Conversely, as discussed later, prophylactic transfusions almost always prevent further vaso-occlusive episodes and pain crises.

329
00:40:11,169 --> 00:40:19,669
Antenatal epidural analgesia may offer benefits for pain (Verstraete, 2012; Winder, 2011).

330
00:40:19,669 --> 00:40:24,419
Long term, affected women can become habituated to narcotics.

331
00:40:24,419 --> 00:40:32,469
This problem is highlighted by the elevated rates of neonatal abstinence syndrome, which is a constellation of withdrawal symptoms .

332
00:40:32,469 --> 00:40:41,832
Rates of covert bacteriuria and acute pyelonephritis are elevated substantively, and screening and treatment for bacteriuria are essential.

333
00:40:41,832 --> 00:40:53,519
If pyelonephritis develops, sickle cells are extremely susceptible to bacterial endotoxin, which can incite dramatic, rapid red cell destruction and suppress erythropoiesis.

334
00:40:53,519 --> 00:40:58,457
Pneumonia, especially due to Streptococcus pneumoniae, is common.

335
00:40:58,457 --> 00:41:08,082
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2020) recommends specific vaccination for those with sickle-cell disease and all asplenic patients.

336
00:41:08,082 --> 00:41:17,732
These are polyvalent pneumococcal, Haemophilus influenzae type B, and meningococcal vaccines (Table 10-7, p. 190).

337
00:41:17,732 --> 00:41:20,782
Pulmonary complications are frequent.

338
00:41:20,782 --> 00:41:31,057
Of these, acute chest syndrome is characterized by pleuritic chest pain, fever, cough, lung infiltrates, hypoxia, and usually bone and joint pain .

339
00:41:31,057 --> 00:41:35,194
It develops in approximately 6 percent of pregnant women .

340
00:41:35,194 --> 00:41:40,294
In addition to symptoms, radiographs show a new pulmonary infiltrate.

341
00:41:40,294 --> 00:41:46,694
The four precipitants are infection, marrow emboli, thromboembolism, and atelectasis .

342
00:41:46,694 --> 00:41:51,619
Bacterial or viral infection causes approximately half of cases.

343
00:41:51,619 --> 00:41:58,269
When acute chest syndrome develops, the mean duration of hospitalization is 10.5 days.

344
00:41:58,269 --> 00:42:05,519
Mechanical ventilation is required in approximately 15 percent, and the mortality rate nears 3 percent .

345
00:42:05,519 --> 00:42:14,682
For nonpregnant adults, some recommend rapid simple transfusion or exchange transfusions to remove the “trigger” for acute chest syndromes .

346
00:42:14,682 --> 00:42:29,357
In a study of nonpregnant patients, Turner and colleagues (2009) reported that exchange transfusion offered no increased benefits compared with simple transfusions, and the former were associated with fourfold greater blood usage.

347
00:42:29,357 --> 00:42:38,369
These results notwithstanding, the American Society of Hematology suggests exchange transfusions instead of simple transfusions.

348
00:42:38,369 --> 00:42:44,444
However, they state this recommendation is conditional due to scarce high-quality evidence .

349
00:42:44,444 --> 00:42:51,207
Women with sickle-cell disease usually have some degree of cardiac dysfunction from ventricular hypertrophy.

350
00:42:51,207 --> 00:42:54,557
Chronic hypertension worsens the dysfunction.

351
00:42:54,557 --> 00:43:02,882
During pregnancy, the basal hemodynamic state characterized by high cardiac output and increased blood volume is augmented .

352
00:43:02,882 --> 00:43:12,907
Although most women tolerate pregnancy without problems, complications such as severe preeclampsia or serious infections may result in ventricular failure .

353
00:43:12,907 --> 00:43:17,882
Heart failure caused by pulmonary hypertension also must be considered.

354
00:43:17,882 --> 00:43:29,182
In 4352 pregnancies in women with sickle-cell syndromes, Chakravarty and associates (2008) reported significantly higher pregnancy complication rates.

355
00:43:29,182 --> 00:43:36,732
Compared with controls, women with sickling disorders had a 63percent rate of nondelivery-related admissions.

356
00:43:36,732 --> 00:43:52,182
They had a 1.8-fold greater incidence of hypertensive disorders—19 percent; a 2.9-fold higher rate of fetal-growth restriction—6 percent; and a 1.7-fold increased cesarean delivery rate—45 percent.

357
00:43:52,182 --> 00:43:59,819
With hemoglobin SC disease, morbidity and mortality rates are appreciably lower than those from sickle-cell anemia.

358
00:43:59,819 --> 00:44:02,657
Indeed, fewer than half of these

359
00:44:02,812 --> 00:44:05,874
women have symptoms before pregnancy.

360
00:44:05,824 --> 00:44:16,312
In our experiences, affected gravidas suffer attacks of severe bone pain and episodes of pulmonary infarction and embolization more commonly than when not pregnant .

361
00:44:16,312 --> 00:44:21,324
Some adverse pregnancy outcomes are shown in Table 59-3.

362
00:44:21,324 --> 00:44:35,787
Prophylactic Red Cell Transfusions Chronic transfusion therapy has the most dramatic benefit on maternal morbidity rates (Benites, 2016; Malinowski, 2015; Vianello, 2018).

363
00:44:35,787 --> 00:44:42,099
It is problematic for universal application because of complications from multiple transfusions.

364
00:44:42,099 --> 00:44:51,412
In an earlier 10-year prospective study at Parkland Hospital, we offered prophylactic transfusions to all pregnant women with sickle-cell syndromes.

365
00:44:51,412 --> 00:45:01,074
Transfusions were given throughout pregnancy to maintain the hematocrit above 25 volumes percent and the portion of hemoglobin S below 60 percent .

366
00:45:01,074 --> 00:45:06,762
Maternal morbidity was minimal, and erythropoiesis suppression was not problematic.

367
00:45:06,762 --> 00:45:12,149
Their outcomes were compared with historical controls who were not routinely transfused.

368
00:45:12,149 --> 00:45:24,662
Overall, maternal morbidity and hospitalization rates were reduced in the transfused group, but perinatal morbidity and mortality rates remained increased because of preterm birth and fetal-growth restriction .

369
00:45:24,662 --> 00:45:36,412
In a multicenter trial, Koshy and coworkers (1988) randomly assigned 72 pregnant women with sickle-cell syndromes to prophylactic or indicated transfusions.

370
00:45:36,412 --> 00:45:45,924
They reported a significant decline in the incidence of painful sickle-cell crises with prophylactic transfusions but no differences in perinatal outcomes.

371
00:45:45,924 --> 00:45:53,237
Because of risks inherent with blood administration, they concluded that prophylactic transfusions were not indicated.

372
00:45:53,237 --> 00:46:07,087
A metaanalysis of 12 studies found prophylactic transfusions improved rates of most adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes, including maternal mortality, pulmonary complications, and perinatal mortality .

373
00:46:07,087 --> 00:46:11,799
Undoubtedly, morbidity from multiple transfusions is significant.

374
00:46:11,799 --> 00:46:19,024
Up to 10 percent of women had a delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction, and infections are a major concern.

375
00:46:19,024 --> 00:46:26,374
Garratty (1997) reviewed 12 studies and found alloimmunization developed in a fourth of women.

376
00:46:26,374 --> 00:46:36,362
Last, from liver biopsies in these women, we found no evidence of transfusion-related iron overload, hemochromatosis, or chronic hepatitis .

377
00:46:36,362 --> 00:46:44,224
Because of what some consider marginal benefits, routine prophylactic transfusions during pregnancy remain controversial .

378
00:46:44,224 --> 00:46:48,337
Current consensus is that their use should be individualized.

379
00:46:48,337 --> 00:46:54,912
With such a practice, approximately 60 percent of women will need transfusions during pregnancy .

380
00:46:54,912 --> 00:47:03,537
Fetal Assessment Perinatal outcomes include increased risks for preterm birth, fetal-growth restriction, and perinatal mortality.

381
00:47:03,537 --> 00:47:10,512
Thus, beginning in the mid-second trimester, serial fetal-growth assessment with sonography is reasonable.

382
00:47:10,512 --> 00:47:19,474
At 32 to 34 weeks’ gestation, weekly antepartum surveillance with biophysical profiles or nonstress test is considered .

383
00:47:19,474 --> 00:47:29,812
Anyaegbunam and colleagues (1991) reported nonreactive stress tests during sickling crises, which resumed reactivity with crisis resolution.

384
00:47:29,812 --> 00:47:36,137
They concluded that transient effects of sickle-cell crisis do not compromise umbilical blood flow.

385
00:47:36,137 --> 00:47:42,512
Of interest, placentas from sickle-cell pregnancies show abnormalities in 69 percent .

386
00:47:42,512 --> 00:47:48,562
Labor and Delivery Management is essentially identical to that for women with cardiac disease .

387
00:47:48,562 --> 00:47:52,124
Women are kept comfortable but not oversedated.

388
00:47:52,124 --> 00:47:55,087
Conduction analgesia is ideal.

389
00:47:55,087 --> 00:47:57,887
Compatible blood should be available.

390
00:47:57,887 --> 00:48:08,349
If a difficult vaginal or cesarean delivery is contemplated and the hematocrit is <20 volumes percent, packed erythrocyte transfusions are administered.

391
00:48:08,349 --> 00:48:14,912
Vaginal delivery is suitable, and cesarean delivery is reserved for obstetrical indications .

392
00:48:14,912 --> 00:48:24,387
In the puerperium, many clinicians do not recommend combination hormonal contraception because of potential adverse vascular and thrombotic effects.

393
00:48:24,387 --> 00:48:32,162
However, one systematic review found that complication rates were not higher with their use in women with sickle-cell syndromes .

394
00:48:32,162 --> 00:48:39,949
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention categorizes combination hormonal contraception, intrauterine devices,

395
00:48:40,108 --> 00:48:48,608
implants, and progestin-only methods as having no risk or as having advantages that generally outweigh theoretical or proven risks .

396
00:48:48,558 --> 00:48:51,083
# Sickle-cell Trait.

397
00:48:51,083 --> 00:48:56,508
The frequency of sickle-cell trait among African Americans averages 8 percent.

398
00:48:56,508 --> 00:49:04,970
Carriers have occasional hematuria, renal papillary necrosis, and hyposthenuria, which is urine of low specific gravity .

399
00:49:04,970 --> 00:49:10,570
Sickle trait may be associated with progression of end-stage renal disease in blacks .

400
00:49:10,570 --> 00:49:27,670
Although controversial, sickle-cell trait does not appear to be associated with increased rates of abortion, perinatal mortality, low birthweight, or pregnancy-induced hypertension (Pritchard, 1973; Wellenstein, 2019; Wilson, 2020).

401
00:49:27,670 --> 00:49:38,708
In one study of more than 5000 women, the incidence of gestational hypertension was significantly elevated, but that of preeclampsia was not (O’Hara, 2020).

402
00:49:38,708 --> 00:49:42,633
Of note, they did not control for chronic hypertension.

403
00:49:42,633 --> 00:49:50,295
One unquestioned relationship is the twofold greater incidence of asymptomatic bacteriuria and urinary infection.

404
00:49:50,295 --> 00:49:56,020
Sickle-cell trait is not considered a deterrent to pregnancy or to hormonal contraception.

405
00:49:56,020 --> 00:49:59,983
# Hemoglobin C and C-β-Thalassemia.

406
00:49:59,983 --> 00:50:09,420
Approximately 2 percent of African-Americans are heterozygous for hemoglobin C, but even if homozygous, hemoglobin C is innocuous .

407
00:50:09,420 --> 00:50:15,683
Only when coinherited with sickle-cell trait to yield hemoglobin SC is the trait problematic.

408
00:50:15,683 --> 00:50:23,970
Pregnancy in women with homozygous hemoglobin CC disease or C-β-thalassemia carries relatively benign associations.

409
00:50:23,970 --> 00:50:29,308
Other than mild to moderate anemia, pregnancy outcomes were not abnormal .

410
00:50:29,308 --> 00:50:33,395
Supplementation with folic acid and iron is indicated.

411
00:50:33,395 --> 00:50:35,895
# Hemoglobin E.

412
00:50:35,895 --> 00:50:43,270
Although uncommon in the United States, hemoglobin E is the second most frequent hemoglobin variant worldwide.

413
00:50:43,270 --> 00:50:47,270
The heterozygous E trait is common in Southeast Asia.

414
00:50:47,270 --> 00:50:59,933
In one study, homozygous hemoglobin E, hemoglobin E plus β-thalassemia, or hemoglobin E trait was identified in 36 percent of Cambodians and 25 percent of Laotians .

415
00:50:59,933 --> 00:51:09,595
Homozygosity for hemoglobin E is associated with little or no anemia, hypochromia, marked microcytosis, or erythrocyte targeting.

416
00:51:09,595 --> 00:51:21,658
With hemoglobin E trait, another study found no increased pregnancy risks other than asymptomatic bacteriuria between 1073 women and 2146 controls .

417
00:51:21,658 --> 00:51:29,733
Conversely, doubly heterozygous E-β-thalassemia is a common cause of severe childhood anemia in Southeast Asia .

418
00:51:29,733 --> 00:51:39,783
In a cohort study of 54 women with singleton pregnancies, a threefold greater risk of preterm birth and fetal-growth restriction was found in affected women .

419
00:51:39,783 --> 00:51:44,720
It is unclear if hemoglobin SE disease is ominous during pregnancy.

420
00:51:44,720 --> 00:51:47,970
# Hemoglobinopathy in the Newborn.

421
00:51:47,970 --> 00:51:56,858
Neonates with homozygous SS, SC, and CC disease can be identified accurately at birth by cord blood electrophoresis.

422
00:51:56,858 --> 00:52:05,995
The United States Health Resources and Services Administration (2020) recommends that all newborns be tested for sickle-cell disease.

423
00:52:05,995 --> 00:52:11,308
In most states, such screening is mandated by law and performed routinely .

424
00:52:11,308 --> 00:52:14,233
# Prenatal Diagnosis.

425
00:52:14,233 --> 00:52:24,770
Inheritance is a concern for the fetus whenever a mother and father carry a gene for abnormal hemoglobins that include S, C, and D or for β-thalassemia trait.

426
00:52:24,770 --> 00:52:30,783
Sickle-cell anemia results from the inheritance of the gene for S hemoglobin from each parent.

427
00:52:30,783 --> 00:52:41,758
In the United States, 1 of 12 AfricanAmericans has sickle-cell trait, which results from inheritance of one gene for hemoglobin S and one for normal hemoglobin A.

428
00:52:41,758 --> 00:52:54,170
The computed incidence of sickle-cell anemia among African-Americans is 1 in 576 (1/12 × 1/12 × 1/4 = 1/576).

429
00:52:54,170 --> 00:52:58,570
The disease is less common in adults because of earlier mortality.

430
00:52:58,570 --> 00:53:04,008
Approximately 1 in 40 African-Americans has the gene for hemoglobin C.

431
00:53:04,008 --> 00:53:17,908
Thus, the theoretical incidence for coinheritance of the gene for hemoglobin S and an allelic gene for hemoglobin C in an AfricanAmerican child is 1 in 2000 (1/12 × 1/40 × 1/4).

432
00:53:17,908 --> 00:53:29,183
β-Thalassemia minor affects about 1 in 40, thus S-β-thalassemia also is found in approximately 1 in 2000 (1/12 × 1/40 × 1/4).

433
00:53:29,183 --> 00:53:33,983
Many tests are available to detect sickle-cell disease antenatally.

434
00:53:33,983 --> 00:53:40,120
Most are DNA based and use chorionic villus samples or amnionic fluid specimens .

435
00:53:40,120 --> 00:53:41,708
Several

436
00:53:41,860 --> 00:53:52,335
mutations that encode hemoglobin S and other abnormal hemoglobins can be detected by targeted mutation analysis and polymerase chain reaction– based techniques .

437
00:53:52,285 --> 00:53:54,647
Thalassemia Syndromes.

438
00:53:54,647 --> 00:53:59,510
Hundreds of mutations affect the genes that control hemoglobin production .

439
00:53:59,510 --> 00:54:11,997
Some of these impair synthesis of one or more of the normal globin peptides and may result in a clinical syndrome characterized by varying degrees of ineffective erythropoiesis, hemolysis, and anemia.

440
00:54:11,997 --> 00:54:16,472
Thalassemias are classified according to the globin that is deficient.

441
00:54:16,472 --> 00:54:25,847
The two major forms involve impaired production or instability of α-globin to cause α-thalassemia or of β-globin to cause β-thalassemia.

442
00:54:25,847 --> 00:54:31,910
Clinically, these can be divided into transfusion dependent and nontransfusion dependent .

443
00:54:31,910 --> 00:54:34,710
# Alpha Thalassemias.

444
00:54:34,710 --> 00:54:41,610
The two α-globin genes, HBA1 and HBA2, are both found on chromosome 16.

445
00:54:41,610 --> 00:54:51,485
Because diploid chromosome sets contains four α-globin genes total, the inheritance of α-thalassemia is more complicated than for β-thalassemia .

446
00:54:51,485 --> 00:54:57,310
Some of the possible genotypes and phenotypes are shown in Table 59-4.

447
00:54:57,310 --> 00:55:00,985
The γ-globin genes are similarly duplicated.

448
00:55:00,985 --> 00:55:10,085
Correspondingly, the normal genotype for diploid cells can be expressed as αα/αα and γγ/γγ.

449
00:55:10,085 --> 00:55:30,460
Of the two main groups of α-thalassemia determinants, α0-thalassemia is mutation of both genes from one chromosome (––/αα), whereas α+thalassemia is mutation of a single gene from one allele (–α/αα heterozygote) or from both alleles (–α/–α homozygote).

450
00:55:30,460 --> 00:55:33,097
TABLE 59-4.

451
00:55:33,097 --> 00:55:44,810
Genotypes and Phenotypes of α-Thalassemia Syndromes The relative frequency of the different α-thalassemia types varies remarkably among racial groups, and all are encountered in Asians.

452
00:55:44,810 --> 00:55:56,272
In those of African descent, although α-thalassemia minor has a frequency approximating 2 percent, hemoglobin H disease is rare and hemoglobin Bart disease is unreported.

453
00:55:56,272 --> 00:56:13,547
This is because Asians usually have α0-thalassemia minor inherited with both gene deletions typically from the same chromosome (––/αα), whereas blacks usually have α+-thalassemia minor in which one gene is deleted from each chromosome (–α/–α).

454
00:56:13,547 --> 00:56:21,535
Pregnancy Important obstetrical aspects of some α-thalassemia syndromes depend on the number of gene deletions in a given woman.

455
00:56:21,535 --> 00:56:27,247
Clinical severity closely correlates with the degree of impaired α-globin synthesis.

456
00:56:27,247 --> 00:56:33,485
The silent carrier state with one gene deletion may be associated with mild microcytic anemia .

457
00:56:33,485 --> 00:56:41,985
Deletion of two genes resulting in α-thalassemia minor is characterized by minimal to moderate hypochromic microcytic anemia.

458
00:56:41,985 --> 00:56:52,035
This is due to either α0- or α+-thalassemia trait, and thus genotypes may be ––/αα or –α/–α, respectively.

459
00:56:52,035 --> 00:56:56,347
Differentiation is possible only by DNA analysis .

460
00:56:56,347 --> 00:57:06,572
Because no other clinical abnormalities accompany either form of α-thalassemia minor, it often goes unrecognized and is usually of no maternal consequence .

461
00:57:06,572 --> 00:57:17,885
The fetus with these forms of thalassemia minor will have hemoglobin Bart (γ4) at birth, but as its levels drop, it is not replaced by hemoglobin H (β4).

462
00:57:17,885 --> 00:57:24,947
Red cells are hypochromic and microcytic, and the hemoglobin concentration is normal to slightly depressed.

463
00:57:24,947 --> 00:57:36,860
Hemoglobin H disease (β4) results from the compound heterozygous state for α0- plus α+-thalassemia with deletion of three of four alpha genes (––/–α).

464
00:57:36,860 --> 00:57:50,272
With only one functional α-globin gene per diploid genome, the newborn will have abnormal red cells containing a mixture of hemoglobin Bart (γ4), hemoglobin H (β4), and hemoglobin A.

465
00:57:50,272 --> 00:57:58,385
Of these three, fetal hemoglobin Bart (γ4) and hemoglobin H (β4) transport oxygen poorly.

466
00:57:58,385 --> 00:58:08,322
The neonate appears normal but soon develops hemolytic anemia as most of the hemoglobin Bart (γ4) is replaced by hemoglobin H (β4).

467
00:58:08,322 --> 00:58:16,410
In adults, their low hemoglobin A production leads to anemia that is moderate to severe and usually worsens during pregnancy.

468
00:58:16,410 --> 00:58:26,397
Inheritance of all four abnormal α-globin genes causes homozygous αthalassemia, which is also called Hb Bart disease and alpha thalassemia major.

469
00:58:26,397 --> 00:58:30,872
Hemoglobin Bart (γ4) is predominantly produced.

470
00:58:30,872 --> 00:58:33,385
This hemoglobin Bart has an

471
00:58:33,556 --> 00:58:42,493
appreciably increased affinity for oxygen, transfers oxygen to end organs poorly, and is incompatible with extended survival.

472
00:58:42,443 --> 00:58:47,781
These fetuses are stillborn or are hydropic and usually die soon after birth.

473
00:58:47,781 --> 00:59:00,481
Sonographic measurement of the fetal cardiothoracic ratio at 12 to 13 weeks’ gestation can be used to identify affected fetuses (Lam, 1999; Zhen, 2015).

474
00:59:00,481 --> 00:59:08,331
Sonographic assessment of myocardial performance—the Tei index— in the first half of pregnancy has been evaluated.

475
00:59:08,331 --> 00:59:12,118
Changes predate hydrops in affected fetuses .

476
00:59:12,118 --> 00:59:16,243
These noninvasive tests may aid pregnancy counseling.

477
00:59:16,243 --> 00:59:19,031
# Beta Thalassemias.

478
00:59:19,031 --> 00:59:25,018
The β-thalassemias stem from impaired β-globin production or α-globin instability.

479
00:59:25,018 --> 00:59:31,181
Genes that encode control of β-globin synthesis are in the δγβgene cluster on chromosome 11 .

480
00:59:31,181 --> 00:59:36,668
More than 150 point mutations in the β-globin gene have been described .

481
00:59:36,668 --> 00:59:44,418
In βthalassemia, β-globin production is decreased, and excess α-globin precipitate to damage cell membranes.

482
00:59:44,418 --> 00:59:49,318
Other forms of β-thalassemias derive from α-globin instability .

483
00:59:49,318 --> 00:59:57,456
The heterozygous trait is β-thalassemia minor, and those most commonly encountered have elevated hemoglobin A2 levels.

484
00:59:57,456 --> 01:00:05,881
This hemoglobin is composed of two α- and two δ-globins, and concentrations are usually more than 3.5 percent.

485
01:00:05,881 --> 01:00:14,668
Hemoglobin F—composed of two α- and two γ-globins— also usually has increased concentrations that exceed 2 percent.

486
01:00:14,668 --> 01:00:25,456
Some patients with heterozygous β-thalassemia minor do not have anemia, and others have mild to moderate anemia characterized by hypochromia and microcytosis.

487
01:00:25,456 --> 01:00:34,443
Homozygous β-thalassemia—also called β-thalassemia major or Cooley anemia—is a serious and frequently fatal disorder.

488
01:00:34,443 --> 01:00:38,318
Hemolysis is intense and leads to severe anemia.

489
01:00:38,318 --> 01:00:50,906
Many patients become transfusion dependent, and the subsequent iron load, along with abnormally greater gastrointestinal iron absorption, leads to hemochromatosis, which is fatal in many cases.

490
01:00:50,906 --> 01:00:58,781
A heterozygous form of β-thalassemia that clinically manifests as thalassemia intermedia produces moderate anemia.

491
01:00:58,781 --> 01:01:02,306
Several treatment schemes treat β-thalassemia.

492
01:01:02,306 --> 01:01:07,368
Stem cell transplantation has been used to treat β-thalassemia major .

493
01:01:07,368 --> 01:01:18,681
Preliminary observations indicate that a combination of thalidomide and hydroxyurea— both contraindicated in pregnancy—may be beneficial to boost hemoglobin production .

494
01:01:18,681 --> 01:01:26,081
Inserting a gene with a lentiviral vector into βglobin has been used to transfect harvested bone marrow stem cells .

495
01:01:26,081 --> 01:01:34,756
Last, luspatercept—a recombinant fusion protein—may enhance erythroid maturation to reduce transfusion requirements .

496
01:01:34,756 --> 01:01:39,481
Pregnancy Iron and folate supplements are given to all affected women.

497
01:01:39,481 --> 01:01:44,193
Those with βthalassemia minor may have mild anemia during pregnancy .

498
01:01:44,193 --> 01:01:49,006
This is caused by ineffective erythropoiesis and not hemolysis.

499
01:01:49,006 --> 01:01:58,668
Shown in Figure 59-3 are comparable red cell survival times in women with β-thalassemia minor compared with those from normally pregnant women.

500
01:01:58,668 --> 01:02:06,593
In some women, anemia will worsen because slightly subnormal erythropoiesis accompanies normal plasma volume expansion.

501
01:02:06,593 --> 01:02:11,168
Fetal-growth restriction has been associated with thalassemia minor .

502
01:02:11,168 --> 01:02:13,843
Figure 59-3.

503
01:02:13,843 --> 01:02:22,906
Erythrocyte-survival times with β-thalassemia minor (black solid line) are comparable to those of normal red cells (shaded area).

504
01:02:22,906 --> 01:02:32,393
Thalassemia major and some of the other severe forms were uncommonly encountered during pregnancy before the advent of transfusion and ironchelation therapy.

505
01:02:32,393 --> 01:02:43,931
In reviews, 63 pregnancies with such management were reported and suffered no serious complications (Aessopos, 1999; Daskalakis, 1998).

506
01:02:43,931 --> 01:02:49,193
Pregnancy is considered reasonably safe if maternal cardiac function is normal.

507
01:02:49,193 --> 01:02:56,543
Transfusions are provided throughout pregnancy to maintain the hemoglobin concentration at 10 g/dL.

508
01:02:56,543 --> 01:02:59,918
This is coupled with surveillance of fetal growth .

509
01:02:59,918 --> 01:03:02,843
# Prenatal Diagnosis.

510
01:03:02,843 --> 01:03:10,431
Diagnosis of α-thalassemia major in the fetus can be accomplished by DNA analysis using molecular techniques .

511
01:03:10,431 --> 01:03:20,368
Fetal diagnosis of hemoglobin Bart (γ4) has been described using capillary electrophoresis or high-performance liquid chromatography techniques .

512
01:03:20,368 --> 01:03:24,043
Molecular genetic testing for HBA1

513
01:03:24,196 --> 01:03:32,033
and HBA2 identifies 90 percent of deletions and 10 percent of point mutations in affected individuals .

514
01:03:31,983 --> 01:03:38,871
Because β-thalassemia major is caused by numerous mutations, prenatal diagnosis is difficult.

515
01:03:38,871 --> 01:03:46,571
For a given individual, targeted mutation analysis requires prior identification of the familial mutation.

516
01:03:46,571 --> 01:03:54,808
The analysis is done using chorionic villus sampling and other techniques discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 344).

517
01:03:54,808 --> 01:04:05,746
Noninvasive testing using cell-free fetal DNA for β-thalassemia diagnosis has been described (Xiong, 2015; Zhang, 2019).

518
01:04:05,746 --> 01:04:12,983
Preimplantation genetic testing allows for identification of affected offspring prior to blastocyst transfer.

519
01:04:12,983 --> 01:04:15,008
Platelet Disorders.

520
01:04:15,008 --> 01:04:17,708
# Thrombocytopenia.

521
01:04:17,708 --> 01:04:26,421
Platelet abnormalities are common and may precede pregnancy, develop coincidentally during pregnancy, or be induced by pregnancy.

522
01:04:26,421 --> 01:04:35,896
Thrombocytopenia—defined by a platelet count <150,000/μL—is identified in nearly 10 percent of gravidas .

523
01:04:35,896 --> 01:04:41,183
Of these cases, 75 percent are gestational thrombocytopenia.

524
01:04:41,183 --> 01:04:46,833
The remainder is due to other causes, and HELLP syndrome is a common one .

525
01:04:46,833 --> 01:04:55,158
Thrombocytopenia may be inherited or idiopathic, acute or chronic, and primary or associated with other disorders .

526
01:04:55,158 --> 01:05:04,958
Of recent importance is thrombocytopenia in pregnancy complicated by COVID-19 infection (Tang, 2020; Zitiello, 2020).

527
01:05:04,958 --> 01:05:07,671
Table 59-5.

528
01:05:07,671 --> 01:05:13,746
Some Causes of Thrombocytopenia in Pregnancy # Gestational Thrombocytopenia.

529
01:05:13,746 --> 01:05:25,046
In two studies of pregnant women, platelet counts falling below the 2.5th percentile were 116,000 and 123,000/µL .

530
01:05:25,046 --> 01:05:30,771
Approximately 1 percent have values <100,000/μL.

531
01:05:30,771 --> 01:05:34,821
Bleeding is only encountered with drastically lower values.

532
01:05:34,821 --> 01:05:50,846
It seems reasonable that a platelet count of <80,000/μL should trigger an evaluation for etiologies other than incidental or gestational thrombocytopenia, which is unlikely to have a platelet count <50,000/μL.

533
01:05:50,846 --> 01:05:57,358
Platelet counts decline normally across pregnancy (Fig. 4-7, p. 62).

534
01:05:57,358 --> 01:06:06,733
With gestational thrombocytopenia, the platelet concentration nadir is usually evident in the third trimester and is thought to stem from hemodilution.

535
01:06:06,733 --> 01:06:14,908
The normal increased splenic mass of pregnancy also may contribute (Maymon, 2006; Reese, 2018).

536
01:06:14,908 --> 01:06:18,208
# Inherited Thrombocytopenias.

537
01:06:18,208 --> 01:06:26,221
Bernard-Soulier syndrome and Glanzmann thrombasthenia lack a platelet membrane glycoprotein, which leads to severe dysfunction .

538
01:06:26,221 --> 01:06:38,283
Moreover, women exposed to fetal platelets carrying this glycoprotein can develop antibodies against this fetal GPIb/IX antigen to cause alloimmune fetal thrombocytopenia .

539
01:06:38,283 --> 01:06:49,146
In 30 pregnancies in 18 women from one review, the primary postpartum hemorrhage rate was 33 percent, and half of women with bleeding required blood transfusion .

540
01:06:49,146 --> 01:06:56,471
The reviewers also described six cases of neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia and two perinatal deaths.

541
01:06:56,471 --> 01:07:03,658
Close monitoring throughout pregnancy and 6 weeks postpartum is critical to avoid potential lifethreatening hemorrhage .

542
01:07:03,658 --> 01:07:12,683
May-Hegglin anomaly is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by thrombocytopenia, giant platelets, and leukocyte inclusions.

543
01:07:12,683 --> 01:07:24,683
In one review of 26 studies containing 75 pregnancies, there were four cases of postpartum hemorrhage, 34 cases of neonatal thrombocytopenia, and two fetal deaths .

544
01:07:24,683 --> 01:07:28,171
# Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura.

545
01:07:28,171 --> 01:07:40,771
The primary form—also termed idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) —is usually caused by a cluster of IgG antibodies directed against one or more platelet glycoproteins.

546
01:07:40,771 --> 01:07:52,046
Antibody-coated platelets are destroyed prematurely in the reticuloendothelial system, especially the spleen (Baucom, 2019; Cooper, 2019).

547
01:07:52,046 --> 01:07:58,883
With ITP, platelet counts range from 10,000 to 100,000/μL .

548
01:07:58,883 --> 01:08:10,558
Although not proven, ITP is probably mediated by autoantibodies directed at platelet-associated immunoglobulins—PAIgG, PAIgM, and PAIgA.

549
01:08:10,558 --> 01:08:15,308
ITP classification is shown in Table 59-6 .

550
01:08:15,308 --> 01:08:20,721
In adults, it usually is a chronic disease that rarely resolves spontaneously.

551
01:08:20,721 --> 01:08:32,521
Secondary forms of immune-mediated chronic thrombocytopenia appear in association with systemic lupus erythematosus, lymphomas, leukemias, and several systemic diseases.

552
01:08:32,521 --> 01:08:37,446
Approximately 2 percent of thrombocytopenic patients have positive

553
01:08:37,612 --> 01:08:44,899
serological tests for lupus, and in some cases, levels of anticardiolipin antibodies are high.

554
01:08:44,849 --> 01:08:53,587
Last, approximately 10 percent of patients with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) have associated thrombocytopenia.

555
01:08:53,587 --> 01:08:56,299
Table 59-6.

556
01:08:56,299 --> 01:09:05,287
Classification of Immune Thrombocytopenia (ITP) Pregnancy Pregnancy does not raise the risk of relapse or worsen active disease.

557
01:09:05,287 --> 01:09:12,162
The estimated incidence of ITP complicating pregnancy approximates 1 case in 10,000 births .

558
01:09:12,162 --> 01:09:20,962
However, it is not unusual for women who have been in clinical remission for several years to have recurrent thrombocytopenia during pregnancy.

559
01:09:20,962 --> 01:09:26,962
Although this may be from closer surveillance, hyperestrogenemia is also implicated.

560
01:09:26,962 --> 01:09:34,799
Therapy is considered if the woman has symptomatic bleeding and the platelet count is below 30,000/μL.

561
01:09:34,799 --> 01:09:39,687
The corrected target level is 50,000/μL .

562
01:09:39,687 --> 01:09:46,324
Primary treatment is corticosteroids, intravenous immune globulin (IVIG), or both .

563
01:09:46,324 --> 01:09:55,924
Initially, prednisone, 1 mg/kg daily, helps suppress the phagocytic activity of the splenic monocyte–macrophage system.

564
01:09:55,924 --> 01:10:02,187
IVIG given in a total dose of 2 g/kg over 2 to 5 days also is effective.

565
01:10:02,187 --> 01:10:08,087
Some immunomodulating agents are avoided in pregnancy due to teratogenicity risks.

566
01:10:08,087 --> 01:10:19,037
Azathioprine (Imuran) and rituximab (Truxima), however, which are used in nonpregnant persons with ITP, have been used for other conditions in pregnancy.

567
01:10:19,037 --> 01:10:32,362
Last, the thrombopoietin-receptor agonists romiplostim (Nplate) and eltrombopag (Promacta) have stimulated responses in some patients (Patras, 2020; Rosa María, 2020).

568
01:10:32,362 --> 01:10:43,024
Prospective observations from the UK Obstetric Surveillance System (UKOSS) of 107 pregnancies were described by Care and associates (2018).

569
01:10:43,024 --> 01:10:48,062
Postpartum hemorrhage occurred in half, and in 20 percent it was severe.

570
01:10:48,062 --> 01:10:54,912
No neonate needed therapy for thrombocytopenia, and no cases of intracranial hemorrhage were noted.

571
01:10:54,912 --> 01:11:02,174
Comont and coworkers (2018) reported on 50 pregnancies in 39 women with ITP.

572
01:11:02,174 --> 01:11:08,674
Half had a platelet count <50,000/μL, but 84 percent were treated.

573
01:11:08,674 --> 01:11:12,412
They concluded that a third of these women were overtreated.

574
01:11:12,412 --> 01:11:21,174
In pregnant women with no response to corticosteroid or IVIG therapy, open or laparoscopic splenectomy may be effective.

575
01:11:21,174 --> 01:11:26,899
In late pregnancy, cesarean delivery may be necessary for surgical exposure.

576
01:11:26,899 --> 01:11:33,249
Improvement usually follows splenectomy in 1 to 3 days and peaks at approximately 8 days.

577
01:11:33,249 --> 01:11:42,099
Fetal and Neonatal Effects Pregnancy complications that are increased with ITP include stillbirth, fetal loss, and preterm birth .

578
01:11:42,099 --> 01:11:49,462
Platelet-associated IgG antibodies cross the placenta, and fetal death from hemorrhage occurs occasionally.

579
01:11:49,462 --> 01:11:57,662
Studies that included more than 800 neonates born to women with ITP cite an intracranial hemorrhage rate <1 percent .

580
01:11:57,662 --> 01:12:01,262
Hemorrhage was not associated with route of delivery.

581
01:12:01,262 --> 01:12:11,212
Investigators concur that fetal and maternal platelet counts lack strong correlation (George, 2009; Hachisuga, 2014).

582
01:12:11,212 --> 01:12:21,587
Because of this, maternal IgG free platelet antibody levels and platelet-associated antibody levels have been evaluated to predict fetal platelet counts.

583
01:12:21,587 --> 01:12:25,562
Again, however, little concurrence with these was found.

584
01:12:25,562 --> 01:12:30,362
Measuring fetal platelet counts by direct sampling is not recommended .

585
01:12:30,362 --> 01:12:39,724
Alloimmune Thrombocytopenia Disparity between maternal and fetal platelet antigens can stimulate maternal production of antiplatelet antibodies.

586
01:12:39,724 --> 01:12:47,512
Such platelet alloimmunization can be severe, and its pathophysiology mirrors that caused by red cell antigens.

587
01:12:47,512 --> 01:12:52,012
This is discussed in Chapter 18 (p. 352).

588
01:12:52,012 --> 01:12:54,687
# Thrombocytosis.

589
01:12:54,687 --> 01:13:04,187
Also called thrombocythemia, thrombocytosis generally is defined as persistent platelet counts >450,000/μL.

590
01:13:04,187 --> 01:13:14,349
Common causes of secondary or reactive thrombocytosis are infection, iron deficiency, trauma, inflammatory diseases, and malignant tumors .

591
01:13:14,349 --> 01:13:22,924
Platelet counts seldom exceed 1 million/μL in these secondary disorders, and prognosis depends on the underlying disease.

592
01:13:22,924 --> 01:13:31,574
Instead, primary or essential thrombocytosis accounts for most cases in which platelet counts exceed 1 million/μL.

593
01:13:31,574 --> 01:13:37,399
It is a clonal disorder often due to an acquired mutation in the JAK2 gene .

594
01:13:37,399 --> 01:13:43,412
Thrombocytosis usually is asymptomatic, but arterial and venous thromboses

595
01:13:43,564 --> 01:13:52,776
may develop, and thrombosis is associated with pregnancy complications (Rabinerson, 2007; Randi, 2014).

596
01:13:52,726 --> 01:13:57,214
First-line treatment is aspirin and possibly hydroxyurea.

597
01:13:57,214 --> 01:14:04,889
Normal pregnancies have been described in women whose mean platelet counts were >1.25 million/μL.

598
01:14:04,889 --> 01:14:15,939
However, in a report of 40 pregnancies in 16 women with essential thrombocythemia, almost half had a spontaneous abortion, fetal demise, or preeclampsia .

599
01:14:15,939 --> 01:14:25,351
In 63 pregnancies in 36 women cared for at the Mayo Clinic, a third had a miscarriage, but other pregnancy complications were uncommon .

600
01:14:25,351 --> 01:14:36,464
In this observational study, aspirin therapy was associated with a significantly lower abortion rate than that in untreated women—1 versus 75 percent, respectively.

601
01:14:36,464 --> 01:14:47,564
Suggested treatments during pregnancy include aspirin, low-molecular-weight heparin, and interferon α (Finazzi, 2012; Vantroyen, 2002).

602
01:14:47,564 --> 01:14:53,214
Interferon α therapy during pregnancy was successful in a review of 11 women.

603
01:14:53,214 --> 01:15:00,414
One women had transient blindness at midpregnancy when her platelet count was 2.3 million/μL .

604
01:15:00,414 --> 01:15:03,289
Thrombotic Microangiopathies.

605
01:15:03,289 --> 01:15:17,926
Although not a primary platelet disorder, some degree of thrombocytopenia accompanies the thrombotic microangiopathies, which include thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) and hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS).

606
01:15:17,926 --> 01:15:25,764
These are characterized by thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolysis, and microvascular thrombosis .

607
01:15:25,764 --> 01:15:31,676
Their similarities to HELLP syndrome allude to their obstetrical ramifications.

608
01:15:31,676 --> 01:15:34,614
# Etiopathogenesis.

609
01:15:34,614 --> 01:15:42,401
Although different causes account for the variable findings within these syndromes, clinically, they frequently are indistinguishable.

610
01:15:42,401 --> 01:15:50,264
Inherited or idiopathic TTP is thought to be caused by antibodies to or a plasma deficiency of ADAMTS13 .

611
01:15:50,264 --> 01:15:58,089
This endothelium-derived metalloprotease cleaves von Willebrand factor (vWF) to decrease its activity.

612
01:15:58,089 --> 01:16:07,264
Conversely, HUS is usually from endothelial damage incited by viral or bacterial infections and is seen primarily in children .

613
01:16:07,264 --> 01:16:13,014
Secondary thrombotic microangiopathies are the most common—94 percent .

614
01:16:13,014 --> 01:16:17,039
A substantial number of all cases are pregnancy related.

615
01:16:17,039 --> 01:16:23,664
Other common causes are malignancies, drugs, transplantations, and autoimmune diseases.

616
01:16:23,664 --> 01:16:32,051
COVID-19 infection has been reported to precipitate TTP (Futterman, 2020; Makatsariya, 2020).

617
01:16:32,051 --> 01:16:39,076
With TTP, intravascular platelet aggregation stimulates a cascade that leads to end-organ failure.

618
01:16:39,076 --> 01:16:46,001
There is endothelial activation and damage, but it is unclear whether this is a consequence or a cause.

619
01:16:46,001 --> 01:16:52,664
Elevated levels of unusually large multimers of vWF are identified with active TTP.

620
01:16:52,664 --> 01:16:58,426
Various defects in the ADAMTS13 gene create differing clinical presentations.

621
01:16:58,426 --> 01:17:07,576
In another mechanism, antibodies raised against ADAMTS13 neutralize its action to cleave vWF multimers during an acute episode.

622
01:17:07,576 --> 01:17:15,114
The result is microthrombi of hyaline material containing platelets and fibrin within arterioles and capillaries.

623
01:17:15,114 --> 01:17:20,926
When sufficient in number or size, these aggregates produce ischemia or infarction.

624
01:17:20,926 --> 01:17:23,526
# Manifestations.

625
01:17:23,526 --> 01:17:31,789
Thrombotic microangiopathies are characterized by thrombocytopenia, fragmentation hemolysis, and organ dysfunction.

626
01:17:31,789 --> 01:17:40,676
TTP has the pentad of thrombocytopenia, hemolytic anemia, fever, renal impairment, and neurological abnormalities.

627
01:17:40,676 --> 01:17:46,839
HUS typically has more profound renal involvement and fewer neurological aberrations .

628
01:17:46,839 --> 01:17:56,039
Thrombocytopenia is usually severe, but fortunately, even with very low platelet counts, spontaneous severe hemorrhage is uncommon.

629
01:17:56,039 --> 01:18:04,901
Microangiopathic hemolysis is associated with moderate to marked anemia, and erythrocyte transfusions are frequently necessary.

630
01:18:04,901 --> 01:18:09,864
The blood smear shows erythrocyte fragmentation and schizocytosis.

631
01:18:09,864 --> 01:18:20,689
Reticulocytes and nucleated red blood cells counts are increased, lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) levels are high, and haptoglobin concentrations are decreased .

632
01:18:20,689 --> 01:18:27,076
Consumptive coagulopathy, although common, is usually subtle and clinically insignificant.

633
01:18:27,076 --> 01:18:29,151
# Treatment.

634
01:18:29,151 --> 01:18:36,926
The cornerstone of treatment for TTP is plasmapheresis with fresh-frozen plasma replacement along with glucocorticoids.

635
01:18:36,926 --> 01:18:45,289
Plasma exchange removes inhibitors and replaces the ADAMTS13 enzyme (George, 2014; Scully,

636
01:18:45,460 --> 01:18:57,710
2019). Treatment with caplacizumab (Cablivi), the anti-vWF immunoglobulin, inhibits the interaction between ultra-large vWF multimers and platelets .

637
01:18:57,660 --> 01:19:03,785
These treatments have remarkably improved outcomes in patients with these formerly fatal syndromes.

638
01:19:03,785 --> 01:19:08,272
Red cell transfusions are imperative for life-threatening anemia.

639
01:19:08,272 --> 01:19:13,360
Treatment is usually continued until the platelet count is normal for 2 days.

640
01:19:13,360 --> 01:19:16,522
Unfortunately, relapses are common.

641
01:19:16,522 --> 01:19:25,247
Additionally, long-term sequelae such as renal impairment can develop (Dashe, 1998; Vesely, 2015).

642
01:19:25,247 --> 01:19:39,510
Treatment for pregnancy-associated HUS, which is complement mediated, is eculizumab (Soliris), the anti-C5 humanized monoclonal antibody (Fakhouri, 2016; Gupta, 2020).

643
01:19:39,510 --> 01:19:41,760
# Pregnancy.

644
01:19:41,760 --> 01:19:52,985
As shown in the Appendix (p. 1228), ADAMTS13 enzyme activity declines across pregnancy by up to 50 percent (Sánchez-Luceros, 2004).

645
01:19:52,985 --> 01:19:58,972
Levels drop even further with preeclampsia, and especially HELLP syndrome.

646
01:19:58,972 --> 01:20:05,110
This is consonant with prevailing opinions that TTP is more commonly seen during pregnancy.

647
01:20:05,110 --> 01:20:16,772
In the Parkland Hospital experience, 11 pregnancies were complicated by these syndromes among nearly 275,000 gravidas—a frequency of 1 in 25,000 .

648
01:20:16,772 --> 01:20:29,535
Some of the disparately higher incidence in pregnancy reported by others may be from the inclusion of women with severe preeclampsia and eclampsia (Hsu, 1995; Magann, 1994).

649
01:20:29,535 --> 01:20:35,810
Differences that usually allow appropriate diagnosis are listed in Table 59-7.

650
01:20:35,810 --> 01:20:43,172
For example, moderate to severe hemolysis is a rather constant feature of thrombotic microangiopathies.

651
01:20:43,172 --> 01:20:49,610
This is seldom severe with preeclampsia, even when complicated by HELLP syndrome .

652
01:20:49,610 --> 01:21:08,847
Moreover, although hyaline microthrombi are seen in the liver with thrombotic microangiopathy, hepatocellular necrosis and elevated serum hepatic transaminase levels, which are characteristic of preeclampsia, are not a common feature (Ganesan, 2011; Sadler, 2010).

653
01:21:08,847 --> 01:21:11,472
Table 59-7.

654
01:21:11,472 --> 01:21:25,697
Some Differential Factors between HELLP Syndrome and Thrombotic Microangiopathiesa The diagnosis of thrombotic microangiopathies, rather than severe preeclampsia, should be clear before initiating therapy.

655
01:21:25,697 --> 01:21:34,472
Unfortunately, recall that determination of ADAMTS13 enzyme activity may be difficult to interpret with HELLP syndrome .

656
01:21:34,472 --> 01:21:42,022
Plasmapheresis is not indicated for preeclampsia-eclampsia complicated by hemolysis and thrombocytopenia.

657
01:21:42,022 --> 01:21:54,547
Importantly, delivery is imperative to reverse the preeclampsia syndrome, but thrombotic microangiopathy is not improved by delivery (Dashe, 1998; Letsky, 2000).

658
01:21:54,547 --> 01:21:59,747
Thrombotic microangiopathy was previously fatal in up to half of mothers.

659
01:21:59,747 --> 01:22:12,647
However, during the past two decades, and coincidental with plasmapheresis and plasma exchange, maternal survival rates have improved dramatically (Go, 2018; Gupta, 2020).

660
01:22:12,647 --> 01:22:23,160
Hunt and associates (2013) reported that TTP accounted for 1 percent of maternal deaths in the United Kingdom from 2003 to 2008.

661
01:22:23,160 --> 01:22:29,785
In a systematic review of 60 cases of pregnancyassociated HUS, two mothers died .

662
01:22:29,785 --> 01:22:36,272
Microangiopathic syndromes are usually recurrent and frequently unassociated with pregnancy.

663
01:22:36,272 --> 01:22:47,035
For example, seven of the 11 women described earlier at Parkland Hospital had recurrent disease either when not pregnant or within the first trimester of a subsequent pregnancy.

664
01:22:47,035 --> 01:22:54,410
George (2009) reported recurrent TTP in only five of 36 subsequent pregnancies.

665
01:22:54,410 --> 01:23:01,922
In 17 women with nonpregnant atypical HUS, recurrence developed in five of 32 pregnancies.

666
01:23:01,922 --> 01:23:04,860
# Long-term Prognosis.

667
01:23:04,860 --> 01:23:16,622
Women who are diagnosed with thrombotic microangiopathy during pregnancy are at risk for serious long-term complications (Gaggl, 2018; George, 2018).

668
01:23:16,622 --> 01:23:21,360
The relapse rate is as high as 40 percent in nonpregnant patients .

669
01:23:21,360 --> 01:23:26,610
The described Parkland experience included a mean 9-year surveillance period .

670
01:23:26,610 --> 01:23:37,847
These women had multiple recurrences; renal disease requiring dialysis, transplantation, or both; severe chronic hypertension; and transfusion-acquired infection.

671
01:23:37,847 --> 01:23:46,260
Two women died remote from pregnancy—one from dialysis complications and one from transfusion-acquired HIV infection.

672
01:23:46,260 --> 01:23:55,185
In the review by Gupta (2020) cited earlier, 15 percent of mothers with atypical HUS had long-term renal failure.

673
01:23:55,185 --> 01:23:56,960
In 19

674
01:23:57,124 --> 01:24:03,024
women with primary atypical HUS, eight progressed to end-stage renal disease .

675
01:24:02,974 --> 01:24:06,036
Inherited Coagulation Defects.

676
01:24:06,036 --> 01:24:15,649
# Hemophilias A and B. Obstetrical hemorrhage may infrequently be the consequence of an inherited defect in a protein that controls coagulation .

677
01:24:15,649 --> 01:24:19,636
Hemophilia and von Willebrand disease are examples.

678
01:24:19,636 --> 01:24:27,424
Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessively transmitted disorder characterized by a marked deficiency of factor VIII.

679
01:24:27,424 --> 01:24:38,586
Severity reflects plasma factor levels and is categorized as mild—levels of 6 to 30 percent; moderate —2 to 5 percent; or severe—less than 1 percent .

680
01:24:38,586 --> 01:24:45,261
It is rare among women compared with men, in whom the heterozygous state is responsible for the disease.

681
01:24:45,261 --> 01:24:54,061
Heterozygous women have diminished factor VIII levels, but almost invariably, the homozygous state is requisite for hemophilia A.

682
01:24:54,061 --> 01:24:59,974
In a few instances, it appears in women spontaneously from a newly mutated gene.

683
01:24:59,974 --> 01:25:07,336
Pregnancy-associated acquired hemophilia A from antibodies may result in severe bleeding-related morbidity .

684
01:25:07,336 --> 01:25:15,461
Christmas disease or hemophilia B is caused by severe deficiency of factor IX and has similar genetic and clinical features.

685
01:25:15,461 --> 01:25:22,299
Pregnancy The risk of obstetrical bleeding with hemophilia is directly related to factor VIII or IX levels.

686
01:25:22,299 --> 01:25:32,986
Affected women have a range of activity that is determined by random X-chromosome inactivation—lyonization—although activity is expected to average 50 percent .

687
01:25:32,986 --> 01:25:37,249
Levels below 10 to 20 percent pose hemorrhage risks.

688
01:25:37,249 --> 01:25:41,436
If levels fall to near zero, this risk is substantial.

689
01:25:41,436 --> 01:25:52,674
Pregnancy does afford some protection, however, because concentrations of both these clotting factors rise appreciably during normal pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1228).

690
01:25:52,674 --> 01:25:57,011
Treatment with desmopressin can stimulate factor VIII release.

691
01:25:57,011 --> 01:26:06,136
Risks are further reduced by avoiding lacerations, minimizing episiotomy use, and maximizing postpartum uterine contractions.

692
01:26:06,136 --> 01:26:11,061
Operative vaginal delivery and cesarean delivery pose bleeding risks.

693
01:26:11,061 --> 01:26:14,549
A few articles describe pregnancy courses.

694
01:26:14,549 --> 01:26:21,586
Kadir and colleagues (1997) reported that 20 percent of carriers had postpartum hemorrhage.

695
01:26:21,586 --> 01:26:30,286
Guy and associates (1992) reviewed five pregnancies in women with hemophilia B, and in all, outcomes were favorable.

696
01:26:30,286 --> 01:26:35,349
They recommended factor IX administration if levels are below 10 percent.

697
01:26:35,349 --> 01:26:40,961
Desmopressin in selected cases has reduced obstetrical bleeding complications .

698
01:26:40,961 --> 01:26:47,411
If a male fetus has hemophilia, the risk of hemorrhage increases after delivery in the neonate.

699
01:26:47,411 --> 01:26:51,124
This is especially true if circumcision is attempted.

700
01:26:51,124 --> 01:26:59,986
Related to preconceptional counseling, most women with hemophilia A or B carry one affected allele and the other X chromosome is normal.

701
01:26:59,986 --> 01:27:06,649
These individuals will have all sons affected by the disease, and half of her daughters will be carriers.

702
01:27:06,649 --> 01:27:15,386
Rarely, a woman may carry two abnormal alleles, in which case all daughters will be carriers and all of her sons will inherit the disease.

703
01:27:15,386 --> 01:27:22,011
Prenatal diagnosis of hemophilia is possible in some families using chorionic villus biopsy .

704
01:27:22,011 --> 01:27:28,261
Preimplantation genetic testing for hemophilia was reviewed by Lavery (2009).

705
01:27:28,261 --> 01:27:31,561
# Factor VIII or IX Inhibitors.

706
01:27:31,561 --> 01:27:38,736
Rarely, antibodies directed against factor VIII or IX are acquired and may lead to life-threatening hemorrhage.

707
01:27:38,736 --> 01:27:46,961
Patients with hemophilia more commonly develop antibodies, and their acquisition in patients without hemophilia is extraordinary.

708
01:27:46,961 --> 01:27:51,136
It has been identified rarely in women during the puerperium .

709
01:27:51,136 --> 01:28:01,374
The prominent clinical feature is severe, protracted, repetitive hemorrhage from the reproductive tract starting a week or so after an apparently uncomplicated delivery .

710
01:28:01,374 --> 01:28:05,961
The activated partial thromboplastin time is markedly prolonged.

711
01:28:05,961 --> 01:28:16,849
Treatment has included multiple blood-component transfusions, immunosuppressive therapy, and attempts at various surgical procedures, especially curettage and hysterectomy.

712
01:28:16,849 --> 01:28:29,311
A recombinant activated factor VII (NovoSeven) stops bleeding in up to 75 percent of patients with these inhibitors (Arruda, 2015; Gibson, 2016).

713
01:28:29,311 --> 01:28:32,186
# Von Willebrand Disease.

714
01:28:32,186 --> 01:28:40,849
At least 20 heterogeneous clinical disorders involve aberrations of factor VIII complex and platelet dysfunction and collectively are

715
01:28:41,020 --> 01:28:44,757
termed von Willebrand disease (vWD).

716
01:28:44,707 --> 01:28:56,132
These abnormalities are the most frequently inherited bleeding disorders, and their prevalence is as high as 1 to 2 percent (Arruda, 2015; Punt, 2020).

717
01:28:56,132 --> 01:29:01,657
Most von Willebrand disease variants are inherited as autosomal dominant traits.

718
01:29:01,657 --> 01:29:07,182
Types I and II are the most common, and type I accounts for 75 percent.

719
01:29:07,182 --> 01:29:11,382
Type III, which is the most severe, is a recessive trait.

720
01:29:11,382 --> 01:29:18,820
Although most cases of acquired vWD develop after age 50 years, some have been reported in pregnant women .

721
01:29:18,820 --> 01:29:29,207
Pathogenesis The von Willebrand factor (vWF) is a series of large, plasma multimeric glycoproteins that form part of the factor VIII complex.

722
01:29:29,207 --> 01:29:36,745
It is essential for normal platelet adhesion to subendothelial collagen and formation of a primary hemostatic plug.

723
01:29:36,745 --> 01:29:42,370
It also plays a major role in stabilizing the coagulant properties of factor VIII.

724
01:29:42,370 --> 01:29:46,932
Factor VIII, a glycoprotein, is synthesized by the liver.

725
01:29:46,932 --> 01:29:55,707
The von Willebrand precursor, which is present in platelets and plasma, is instead synthesized by endothelium and megakaryocytes.

726
01:29:55,707 --> 01:30:03,582
The von Willebrand factor antigen (vWF:Ag) is the antigenic determinant measured by immunoassays.

727
01:30:03,582 --> 01:30:13,370
Symptomatic women typically present with easy bruising, epistaxis, mucosal hemorrhage, heavy menses, and excessive bleeding with trauma or surgery.

728
01:30:13,370 --> 01:30:18,995
The classic autosomal dominant forms usually cause symptoms in the heterozygous state.

729
01:30:18,995 --> 01:30:38,770
With vWD, laboratory features often include a prolonged bleeding time, prolonged partial thromboplastin time, decreased vWF antigen levels, decreased factor VIII immunological and coagulationpromoting activity, and inability of platelets from an affected person to react to various stimuli.

730
01:30:38,770 --> 01:30:47,645
Although most patients with vWD have heterozygous variants and associated minor bleeding complications, the disease can be severe.

731
01:30:47,645 --> 01:30:52,695
Moreover, homozygous offspring develop serious clotting dysfunction.

732
01:30:52,695 --> 01:31:01,707
Chorionic villus sampling with DNA analysis to detect the missing genes has been described, but the specific genetic mutation must be known.

733
01:31:01,707 --> 01:31:09,445
Some authorities recommend cesarean delivery to avoid trauma to a possibly affected fetus if the mother has severe disease.

734
01:31:09,445 --> 01:31:19,657
Pregnancy During normal pregnancy, maternal levels of both factor VIII and vWF antigen increase substantively (Appendix, p. 1228).

735
01:31:19,657 --> 01:31:33,907
Because of this, pregnant women with vWD often develop normal levels of factor VIII coagulant activity and vWF antigen, although their measured bleeding time still may be prolonged (Delbrück, 2019).

736
01:31:33,907 --> 01:31:39,532
If factor VIII activity is very low or if there is bleeding, treatment is recommended.

737
01:31:39,532 --> 01:31:45,607
Desmopressin by infusion transiently increases factor VIII and vWF levels .

738
01:31:45,607 --> 01:31:52,807
With significant bleeding, 15 or 20 units of cryoprecipitate are transfused every 12 hours.

739
01:31:52,807 --> 01:32:01,782
Alternatively, factor VIII concentrates (Alphanate, Humate-P) that contain high-molecular-weight vWF multimers may be given.

740
01:32:01,782 --> 01:32:10,132
Lubetsky and colleagues (1999) described continuous infusion with Humate-P in a woman during a vaginal delivery.

741
01:32:10,132 --> 01:32:22,270
According to Chi and coworkers (2009), conduction analgesia can be provided safely if coagulation defects have corrected or if hemostatic agents are administered prophylactically.

742
01:32:22,270 --> 01:32:28,995
Pregnancy outcomes in women with vWD are generally good, but postpartum hemorrhage is common.

743
01:32:28,995 --> 01:32:36,695
In one systematic review, with more than 800 deliveries, the postpartum hemorrhage incidence was 33 percent .

744
01:32:36,695 --> 01:32:47,032
In a database review of more than 2200 deliveries complicated by vWD, this incidence was only about 5 percent (O’Brien, 2020).

745
01:32:47,032 --> 01:32:57,270
From two reviews, postpartum hemorrhage may be primary and at the time of delivery, or it may have a delayed onset (Makhamreh, 2021a,b).

746
01:32:57,270 --> 01:33:00,332
# Other Factor Deficiencies.

747
01:33:00,332 --> 01:33:08,145
In general, the activity of most procoagulant factors rises across pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1228).

748
01:33:08,145 --> 01:33:12,782
Factor VII deficiency is a rare autosomal recessive disorder.

749
01:33:12,782 --> 01:33:20,832
Levels of this factor increase during normal pregnancy, but these may rise only mildly in women with factor VII deficiency .

750
01:33:20,832 --> 01:33:30,470
A systematic review of 94 births found no difference in postpartum hemorrhage rates with or without prophylaxis with recombinant factor VIIa .

751
01:33:30,470 --> 01:33:33,532
Factor X or Stuart-Prower factor

752
01:33:33,700 --> 01:33:38,425
deficiency is rare and inherited as an autosomal recessive trait.

753
01:33:38,375 --> 01:33:43,700
Factor X levels typically rise by 50 percent during normal pregnancy.

754
01:33:43,700 --> 01:33:47,525
Despite this, adverse pregnancy outcome are common.

755
01:33:47,525 --> 01:33:54,587
Spiliopoulos and coworkers (2019) reported 31 pregnancies in 19 women.

756
01:33:54,587 --> 01:34:00,850
They described a high rate of preterm birth, perinatal mortality, and postpartum hemorrhage.

757
01:34:00,850 --> 01:34:09,312
Conversely, Nance and colleagues (2012) described 24 pregnancies, of which 18 resulted in a healthy baby.

758
01:34:09,312 --> 01:34:16,662
Treatment is with plasma-derived factor X, fresh-frozen plasma, or prothrombin complex concentrates.

759
01:34:16,662 --> 01:34:22,612
Factor XI deficiency is inherited as an autosomal recessive trait in most families.

760
01:34:22,612 --> 01:34:28,950
It manifests as severe disease in homozygotes but only as a minor defect in heterozygotes.

761
01:34:28,950 --> 01:34:34,162
It is most prevalent in Ashkenazi Jews and is rarely seen in pregnancy.

762
01:34:34,162 --> 01:34:42,725
In one review of 105 pregnancies from 33 affected women, 70 percent had an uneventful pregnancy and delivery .

763
01:34:42,725 --> 01:34:53,087
Authors recommended peripartum treatment with factor XI concentrate for cesarean delivery and advised against epidural analgesia unless factor XI is given.

764
01:34:53,087 --> 01:35:00,437
From another review, factor XI levels and bleeding severity correlated poorly in women with severe deficiency .

765
01:35:00,437 --> 01:35:12,525
Wiewel-Verschueren and associates (2016) performed a systematic review of 27 studies with 372 women and reported that 18 percent had postpartum hemorrhage.

766
01:35:12,525 --> 01:35:19,012
Factor XII deficiency is another autosomal recessive disorder that rarely complicates pregnancy.

767
01:35:19,012 --> 01:35:25,275
A greater incidence of thromboembolism is encountered in nonpregnant patients with this deficiency.

768
01:35:25,275 --> 01:35:32,650
Factor XIII deficiency is an autosomal recessive trait and may be associated with maternal intracranial hemorrhage .

769
01:35:32,650 --> 01:35:41,087
In their review, Kadir and associates (2009) cited an increased risk of recurrent miscarriage and placental abruption.

770
01:35:41,087 --> 01:35:45,062
It has also been reported to cause umbilical cord bleeding .

771
01:35:45,062 --> 01:35:47,900
Treatment is fresh frozen plasma.

772
01:35:47,900 --> 01:35:57,887
Naderi and colleagues (2012) described 17 successful pregnancies in women receiving weekly prophylaxis with factor XIII concentrate.

773
01:35:57,887 --> 01:36:05,450
Fibrinogen abnormalities—either qualitative or quantitative—also may cause coagulation abnormalities.

774
01:36:05,450 --> 01:36:15,275
Autosomally inherited abnormalities usually involve the formation of a functionally defective fibrinogen— commonly referred to as dysfibrinogenemia.

775
01:36:15,275 --> 01:36:21,862
Familial hypofibrinogenemia and sometimes afibrinogenemia are infrequent recessive disorders.

776
01:36:21,862 --> 01:36:28,800
Our experience suggests that hypofibrinogenemia represents a heterozygous autosomal dominant state.

777
01:36:28,800 --> 01:36:37,137
The thrombin-clottable protein level in these patients typically ranges from 80 to 110 mg/dL when nonpregnant.

778
01:36:37,137 --> 01:36:47,325
Cai and coworkers (2018) described successful outcomes in affected women in whom fibrinogen or plasma infusions were given throughout pregnancy.

779
01:36:47,325 --> 01:36:49,325
Thrombophilias.

780
01:36:49,325 --> 01:36:57,987
Several important regulatory proteins act as inhibitors at strategic sites in the coagulation cascade to maintain blood fluidity.

781
01:36:57,987 --> 01:37:03,612
Inherited deficiencies of these inhibitory proteins are caused by gene mutations.

782
01:37:03,612 --> 01:37:10,862
Because they may be associated with recurrent thromboembolism, they are collectively referred to as thrombophilias.

783
01:37:10,862 --> 01:37:21,062
These are discussed in Chapter 55 (p. 976) and reviewed by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020).

